{"entity": "Effect Of Phase Change Material On The Thermal Performance Of Photovoltaic Systems: A Review", "type": "content", "description": "A 2016 review paper examining the effect of Phase Change Material (PCM) on the thermal performance of photovoltaic (PV) systems, aggregating multiple studies on PCM thermal management of PV panels"}
{"entity": "PV Panel", "type": "artifact", "description": "A PV Panel, commonly known as a photovoltaic or solar panel, is a device designed to absorb solar energy and generate electricity through photovoltaic conversion. Structurally, it consists of multiple layers that capture solar irradiation, typically featuring a front glass surface and a back aluminum surface designed to dissipate heat to the ambient air. Physically, an experimental PV Panel often presents a dark blue, grid-textured top surface with specific dimensions of 300 mm in width and 700 mm in length. Commercial iterations of these panels typically exhibit Fill Factor values ranging from 0.70 to 0.85. 

In experimental and operational settings, the PV Panel frequently serves as a standard, conventional reference module. It is utilized to establish a baseline performance metric for comparing electrical efficiency and power output against enhanced PV-PCM (Phase Change Material) panels. To measure solar radiation and temperature distribution accurately, the reference panels are often tested in pairs under identical meteorological conditions. These panels are installed outside facing south to maximize solar exposure and are evaluated at various adjustable tilt angles—specifically 10°, 20°, 30°, and 40°. The overarching studies also evaluate these reference baselines against varying PCM thicknesses and cooling fin geometries to determine optimal thermal regulation strategies.

The performance of the conventional, ambient air-cooled PV Panel is defined by several key operational metrics. Under specific experimental conditions involving a 40° tilt angle and 841 W/m² of solar radiation, the reference panel achieves a maximum electrical efficiency of 13.5%. Operating under these exact conditions, the panel yields a maximum power output of 190.3 W. Across varying operational parameters within the same studies, the reference panel consistently registers an average power output of 165 W, while specific experimental models are generally rated at approximately 250 W of power.

Because elevated operating temperatures negatively impact the PV Panel's electrical efficiency and overall performance, managing its thermal behavior is a primary focus of ongoing research. As the panel's surface receives solar irradiation, it undergoes simultaneous photovoltaic conversion and thermal heating. During experimental comparisons, the reference panel's average surface temperature has been recorded at 39.18°C in numerical simulations and 40.92°C in laboratory experiments. To mitigate excessive heat and optimize electrical output, researchers actively analyze various cooling methods. By comparing the standard ambient-cooled PV Panel against systems integrating Phase Change Materials, researchers can evaluate the effectiveness of enhanced heat dissipation techniques, ensuring the panel's temperature is effectively managed and its electricity generation capabilities are maximized.<SEP>A photovoltaic panel with dimensions of 650 mm x 350 mm x 25 mm, used as the base component in the systems.<SEP>A conventional photovoltaic panel used as a reference system without PCM in the comparative study.<SEP>Photovoltaic panels that are subjected to cooling using thermal management systems.<SEP>Photovoltaic module, an example hot device cooled by the hybrid loop under pulsed or cyclic thermal loads.<SEP>Photovoltaic panel; its efficiency can be improved through PCM-based thermal management, and it is the subject of cooling case studies (passive and active with heat exchanger) in the surrounding research paper.<SEP>5cm thick photovoltaic panel cooled via a channel loaded with gravel (porous media) acting as cooling medium beneath it"}
