{"entity": "Photovoltaic Module", "type": "artifact", "description": "The Photovoltaic Module is a solar energy conversion device that generates electricity from incident sunlight. Its electrical performance, power output, and conversion efficiency are highly dependent on operating parameters, most notably temperature. A defining thermal characteristic of the photovoltaic module is that often more than 70% of the incident solar spectrum is absorbed as heat rather than converted into electrical work, meaning that its energy distribution and thermal losses must be actively managed to maintain system stability. Under high irradiation, the module's temperature can exceed optimal efficiency thresholds, which degrades its conversion efficiency and reduces its daily energy yield.

In a PCM-based cooling optimization case study, the Photovoltaic Module serves as the "hot device." Within this case study, it is characterized by an energy state E_d, a thermal capacitance C_d of 4580 J/°C, an absorptivity of 0.7, a surface area A_s of 0.8 m², and a conversion efficiency of approximately 20%. Cooling the module lowers its operating temperature and increases its daily energy production.

The thermal regulation of the Photovoltaic Module is the central target of phase change material (PCM)-based cooling optimization strategies. Cited studies integrate photovoltaic modules with phase change materials and apply hybrid approaches, such as finned-PCM configurations and nanoparticle-enhanced PCM, to achieve high-efficiency passive cooling. These PCM-based thermal management techniques enhance both the module's electricity yield and its operational lifespan. In economic evaluations, conventional bare photovoltaic modules serve as the benchmark against which the levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) of cooled modules is compared."}
{"entity": "OM-29 PCM", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "A phase change material used with an aluminum plate for PV cooling, resulting in a 10.35 °C temperature reduction and a 30% increase in power output."}
{"entity": "Photovoltaic Panels", "type": "artifact", "description": "Photovoltaic panels are solar energy conversion devices designed to convert sunlight directly into electricity. They typically appear as large, rectangular panels featuring dark blue, grid-like surfaces characteristic of silicon cells, and are commonly mounted side-by-side on A-frame stands. As devices whose electrical efficiency is routinely calculated and analyzed—including the quantification of associated uncertainty—photovoltaic panels serve as central subjects of experimental and analytical research.

The electrical efficiency and overall performance of photovoltaic panels are heavily influenced by their operating temperatures, which makes them a primary focus of passive cooling research and the target of various cooling techniques aimed at improving electrical output. Both the efficiency and longevity of these panels can be enhanced through active cooling that reduces their operating temperatures, and they also function as an application area for hybrid thermal management strategies based on PCM-driven active cooling approaches.

A prominent method of thermal management involves the integration of Phase Change Materials (PCMs). In specialized PV-PCM systems, the surface temperature of photovoltaic panels is actively regulated by the PCM to prevent overheating, effectively managing and reducing the peak temperatures reached during operation, thereby enhancing operational efficiency. These panels have been experimentally tested both with and without PCM integration across various tilt angles and solar radiation intensities to evaluate performance differences. Notably, the application of NePCMs (nano-enhanced Phase Change Materials) has been shown to substantially boost panel performance, resulting in increased power output and improved overall energy efficiency.<SEP>Solar energy conversion devices whose performance is improved through cooling techniques, including phase change material based thermal regulation and various other cooling methods."}
{"entity": "PV-PCM Panel", "type": "artifact", "description": "The PV-PCM Panel, also known as a Photovoltaic-Phase Change Material panel, is a hybrid photovoltaic system designed to enhance overall performance through passive cooling and thermal management. In this configuration, phase change material (PCM) is integrated onto the back surface or rear side of the solar panel, often achieved by directly mounting the photovoltaic panel on top of a dedicated PCM container, with experimental setups utilizing a PCM thickness of 3 cm. This structural integration serves a critical cooling function by utilizing the thermal regulation properties of the PCM to absorb heat, effectively lowering the operating surface temperature of the solar panel. During operation and evaluation, the panel demonstrates non-uniform temperature distribution and temperature gradients along its height and across its front face.

By mitigating operating temperatures, the PV-PCM Panel directly improves the system's electrical output and overall energy conversion efficiency. The integrated system consistently outperforms standard reference PV panels across the radiation spectrum. Under specific testing conditions, such as an 841 W/m² solar radiation level, the PV-PCM Panel achieves a notable electrical efficiency of 15.4%.

In scientific and research contexts, the PV-PCM Panel is frequently utilized as an experimental subject to evaluate its thermal behavior, regulation efficacy, and performance enhancements. To accurately assess temperature variations and distribution, these panels are equipped with strategically placed temperature sensors. Researchers systematically evaluate the panel's performance using various types of phase change materials—such as PCM1, PCM2, and PCM3—and across a range of specific tilt angles. Experimental studies have comprehensively tested these integrated panels at tilts of 10°, 20°, 30°, and 40° to analyze how different physical orientations impact the thermal management effectiveness and overall efficiency of the system."}
{"entity": "PCM", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "PCM is an abbreviation referring to two distinct concepts: Phase Change Material and Phase Change Memory.

As a Phase Change Material, PCM denotes substances that store and release large latent heat at nearly constant temperature through reversible phase transitions, prized for isothermal behavior and high heat storage density. PCMs span gas-solid, gas-liquid, solid-solid, and solid-liquid types, classified as organic (paraffins, fatty acids, biobased esters), inorganic (salts, salt hydrates), eutectic, and polymeric (e.g., PEG), selected by melting range, latent heat, density, conductivity, and peak temperature; hysteresis is common. Paraffin PCMs melt from white semi-solids into dark amber translucent liquids. Challenges include low thermal conductivity (restricting heat flux and prolonging charge/discharge), leakage, supercooling, phase separation, volume change, low chemical and thermal stability, high flammability, and cycling degradation—mitigated by encapsulation, porous matrices, nanostructures, metal foams or fibers, silica or bio-based form-stable formats, and coated ceramic scaffolds; a ceramic-scaffold composite PCM supports aerospace electronics thermal management. Modeled as thermal batteries (capacity C_pcm = m_pcm·L_f), PCMs clip temperature peaks of hot devices, operating standalone or with heat exchangers.

Applications include PV-PCM systems absorbing panel waste heat by day and solidifying at night, cutting temperatures up to 42% and enhancing electrical efficiency; BIPVT paraffin packs marginally boosting electrical output while reducing thermal efficiency 14.2%; building envelopes damping daily oscillations (a 1 cm layer balancing cost/performance over 35 years); cementitious fillers lengthening setting and lowering conductivity, porosity, density, and strength; electronics heat sinks dropping temperatures 10.8/50.1/69.8 °C at 5/10/15 W, though capacity may exhaust after ~34 minutes; battery thermal management; cold storage (−19 °C PCM outperforming water and ice); waste-heat recovery; and latent-heat thermal energy storage, including PCM filling TES plate lattices and PCM-metal foam composites whose melting evolution is studied. Clinoptilolite-hydrocarbon composites (melting ~27–28 °C, enthalpy 190 J/g) enable thermoregulating textiles. In lime renders and mortars, PCM is dosed as form-stable, microencapsulated slurry, or powder (typically 20% of lime weight); powder types fail freeze-thaw catastrophically, and multi-PCM mortars enable multi-stage regulation. Nano-PCMs with nanoparticles (CuO, alumina, titania, silver, Fe3O4) show melting, charging, and discharging dependent on nanomaterial type, concentration, and cycling; hot PCM solidifies nearly isothermally (~70 °C to ~50 °C), with pure paraffin as benchmark.

In solar distillation, PCM acts as latent heat storage—absorbing energy under strong radiation to slow temperature rise and releasing heat gradually in afternoon and night—sustaining evaporation and freshwater production during cloudy or nighttime conditions, improving efficiency and productivity (66.18% and 54.04% in prior research); a 60–65 °C melting PCM (including beeswax) maintains TaPCM temperature (65 °C vs 60 °C at 15:00) after radiation declines.

Distinctly, PCM also abbreviates Phase Change Memory, a non-volatile memory technology also called PRAM and Ovonic Unified Memory. Fast-switching chalcogenide candidates are identified via thermodynamic analysis, with GST as the standard comparison material; these materials are electrically pulsed between amorphous and crystalline states, and wide-bandgap Sb2S3 enables large-area phase switching in reconfigurable photonics, including PCMs capped with 80 nm Al2O3 and crystallized by annealing and electrical switching.<SEP>Phase-change material whose heating involves substantial heat generation, requiring prevention of thermal crosstalk to the semiconductor SOA to minimize thermal interference with laser performance.<SEP>The phase-change material region of the photonic device, represented together with the external cavity by the upper rectangular structure in the thermal simulation."}
