{"entity": "Photovoltaic Systems", "type": "artifact", "description": "Photovoltaic Systems are renewable energy technologies designed to directly convert light into electricity using solar cells. As solar power generation systems, their primary function is to harness solar energy. However, a significant technical challenge they face during energy generation is that their performance and overall energy efficiency are negatively impacted by high operating temperatures. To counteract this thermal degradation and maintain optimal power output, these systems require effective thermal management and sustainable cooling techniques.

Because of these inherent thermal challenges, Photovoltaic Systems are the frequent subject of extensive academic research, comprehensive review articles, and laboratory testing. The primary goal of this surrounding academic context is to optimize their operation by developing and testing various cooling techniques. Researchers broadly categorize the thermal management solutions applied to Photovoltaic Systems into active and passive methods. Specific approaches include the use of radiative cooling, heat pipes, and heat exchangers. 

Among the various cooling strategies, the application of Phase Change Materials (PCMs) is particularly prominent. Academic reviews frequently focus on the integration of PV/PCM systems, alongside hybrid Photovoltaic/Thermal (PV/T) systems, which utilize phase change materials and heat exchangers for enhanced thermal regulation. Furthermore, advanced cooling solutions—such as the integration of Nano-enhanced Phase Change Materials (NePCMs) and the use of finned containers—have been shown to significantly enhance the operation of solar PV panels. 

By employing these advanced cooling and thermal management solutions, Photovoltaic Systems successfully overcome temperature-related inefficiencies. The ongoing development and laboratory testing of these techniques ensure that Photovoltaic Systems achieve sustained performance, resulting in increased power output and improved overall energy efficiency.<SEP>Solar power systems that require active thermal management to maintain optimal performance under hot climate conditions.<SEP>Systems that convert solar energy to electricity, also referred to as PV systems, which benefit from PCM thermal regulation showing efficiency improvements of 3.8%-6.5% and temperature reductions up to 13.1°C<SEP>Systems whose thermal performance is investigated and improved using metal foams and phase change materials.<SEP>Solar energy systems (PV) operating from 20°C to 75°C, showing efficiency drops of 0.03-0.06% per 1°C rise in temperature; applications where thermal management is critical.<SEP>Solar energy conversion systems whose module thermal behavior, operating temperatures, and temperature-dependent efficiency are the subject of the cited research."}
