Analysis: This chart presents Figure 7 from the study, displaying the temporal variations of PV solar cell temperature over a 24-hour period (starting from 6 AM) for four different system configurations: a reference PV system without PCM and three PV-PCM systems with PCM thicknesses of 30 mm, 40 mm, and 50 mm. The x-axis represents time in hours, while the y-axis shows temperature in Kelvin. The chart illustrates several critical thermal behaviors: (1) the reference PV system reaches a peak temperature of 322.2 K around noon, closely following the solar radiation profile; (2) all three PV-PCM systems show reduced peak temperatures due to the PCM's latent heat absorption once the cell temperature exceeds the PCM melting point (307 K for RT35HC paraffin wax); (3) peak temperatures in PV-PCM systems are delayed to approximately 14:00, with values of 319 K (30 mm PCM) and 317.9 K (both 40 mm and 50 mm PCM); (4) the PCM's drawback is evident in the evening/nighttime period where higher final temperatures persist due to incomplete solidification—final temperatures are 299.2 K, 300.5 K, and 302.2 K for 30 mm, 40 mm, and 50 mm PCM respectively. The 40 mm PCM configuration is identified as optimal, balancing peak temperature reduction (4.3 K drop) with acceptable nighttime heat dissipation. This figure directly supports the section 4.2 discussion on thermal simulation results and provides quantitative evidence for PCM effectiveness in PV cooling applications in hot climate conditions (Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam)."}
