Analysis: This chart presents two complementary thermal performance comparison plots (Figures 7 and 8) illustrating solar cell temperature variations over a 24-hour period (starting from 6 AM GMT+7) for different PV and PV-PCM system configurations under Ho Chi Minh City weather conditions (February 15, 2024).

Figure 7 compares four configurations: a reference PV system without PCM and three PV-PCM systems with PCM thicknesses of 30 mm, 40 mm, and 50 mm. The reference system reaches a peak temperature of 322.2 K around noon, following solar radiation patterns. PV-PCM systems demonstrate delayed and reduced peak temperatures (~319 K for 30 mm, ~317.9 K for both 40 mm and 50 mm) occurring around 14:00, as PCM absorbs heat when cell temperature exceeds the PCM melting point (307 K). Final temperatures at day's end vary: 299.2 K (30 mm), 300.5 K (40 mm), and 302.2 K (50 mm), indicating thicker PCM retains more heat overnight.

Figure 8 compares the 40 mm PCM configuration with internal cooling fins (one fin and two fins, each 2 mm thick × 40 mm long) against the reference and no-fin systems. Adding fins significantly enhances thermal performance: one fin reduces peak to 313.6 K, while two fins achieve 312.2 K—a 10 K reduction from the reference system.

The charts demonstrate PCM's effectiveness in reducing peak cell temperatures but also reveal the drawback of elevated nighttime temperatures during PCM solidification. Internal fins address this limitation by improving heat dissipation. These visualizations support the broader research finding that PV-PCM systems with optimized fins can substantially enhance solar cell efficiency in hot climate conditions."}
