1.2. The Thermal Penalty in PV Systems
While solar PV technology effectively harvests incident photons, its operational effi-
ciency is intrinsically constrained by a severe “thermal penalty.” When exposed to high
solar irradiance, elevated operating temperatures precipitate a cascading degradation of
both electrical efficiency and long-term material integrity [5]. For conventional monocrys-
talline and polycrystalline silicon modules, the temperature coefficient ranges from −0.4%
to −0.5% per 1 ◦C increase above the standard test condition (STC) of 25 ◦C. Emerging
next-generation technologies, such as perovskite solar cells, exhibit an even more acute ther-
mal sensitivity, rendering thermal management a critical requirement for their commercial
viability [6].
Beyond immediate power loss, chronic thermal stress significantly accelerates material
degradation within the module architecture. Sustained operating temperatures exceeding
60 ◦C induce severe thermochemical breakdown of the Ethylene Vinyl Acetate (EVA) encap-
sulant. This leads to EVA “yellowing,” which significantly attenuates optical transmittance
and promotes severe delamination at the glass-encapsulant interface [7–9]. Furthermore,
severe thermal cycling exacerbates micro-cracking in the silicon wafers and accelerates the
degradation of solder joints, radically shortening the operational lifespan and increasing
the levelized cost of energy (LCOE) [10,11].
1.3. Evolution of PV Cooling Technologies and the Emergence of PCMs
To counteract these formidable thermal barriers, various cooling paradigms have been
developed. Active cooling techniques, such as forced water circulation or air convection,
exhibit robust heat removal capacities but have fundamental limitations: they require con-
tinuous parasitic power (e.g., pumps and fans), suffer from high maintenance complexities,
and involve high capital expenditures [12,13]. Conversely, phase change materials (PCMs)
have emerged as the widely researched passive thermal buffering solution. Operating as
zero-energy thermodynamic sinks, PCMs leverage their high latent heat of fusion to absorb
transient peak thermal loads, maintaining the PV module in an isothermal state without
consuming electrical power or requiring moving parts [14,15].
