{"entity": "Thermal Conductivity", "type": "concept", "description": "Thermal conductivity, denoted k or λ and measured in W/(m·K), is a fundamental thermophysical property quantifying heat-conduction ability and governing conductive transfer via Fourier's law. It depends, under steady-state conditions, mainly on structural topology and geometric configuration; is influenced by pore geometry in porous media; is temperature-dependent; and changes significantly during phase transitions.

It is intrinsically low in phase change materials (PCMs)—typically 0.2–0.7 W/(m·K), with paraffins near 0.15–0.2—restricting heat exchange, latent heat utilization, and charging/discharging rates. Enhancement techniques, studied in solid and liquid phases, employ nanofillers (Al₂O₃, TiO₂, MWCNTs, carbon nanotubes, graphene, nanoplatelets, nanofibers), suspended additives, stationary structures (solid matrices, porous media described by equation models), metal fins, copper foams, expanded graphite, carbon/graphite hybrids, metal foams, and porous silica supports. Gains reach 56.80% with 1% TiO₂ in paraffin, 40% in nanoPCMs at 20 °C, 13.15% efficiency improvement at 0.3 versus 0.265 W/(m·K), and 576% for carbon-based composites. Enhancement is nonlinear with concentration and depends on dispersion and nanoparticle content; high conductivity alone does not guarantee optimal performance, and gains must be weighed against increased viscosity. Its augmentation and volume fraction govern heat-transfer effectiveness in PCM-integrated cooling systems and heatsinks, making it the key thermal-management target, a critical limitation of conventional PCMs, and a focus for future reliable composites.

In cementitious materials, cement–lime pastes lose 5–11% (up to 47.4%); BA cement replacement has no effect; mortars gain 3–14% by aggregate type, lightweight lowest (1.04–1.15; 1.08 with paraffin P21). Form-stable multi-PCM lime mortars fall slightly below references, while optimized mortars show moderate temperature dependence (−5 to 35 °C); conductivity and microstructural differences—not transition temperatures—explain performance, higher values giving steeper transient curves. In phase-change memory, it sets Joule-heating temperature and, with heat capacity, programming temperature—hence melting current is not proportional to melting temperature—and falls upon crystallization via vibrational softening; in GST/Sb₂Te₃ superlattices it reflects bonding character and nanoscale heterogeneity, thermal boundary resistance confining heat and reducing energy use. In PV-PCM systems it spans high (silicon, aluminium) to extremely low (RT35 HC PCM); 3D-printed cellular insulation reaches its lowest λ (0.0289 W/(m·K); Kelvin structure, 30% PCM) via porosity and reduced bulk density. It also plays a significant role in nanofluid characterization and is highly sensitive to nanostructure in synthetic diamond.

Thermal conductivity is measured via transient plane source (~5% accuracy), heat transfer analysers, T-history, Lee's Disc, and FDTR with femtosecond pump-probe techniques on disc specimens (−5 to 35 °C; solid, transitioning, melted states); it can be co-measured with thermal effusivity via a momentary constant heat source, is the most-studied nano-PCM property, and is discussed in the review's 'Conclusion and Future Directions'."}
