{"entity1": "Nanoparticles", "entity2": "Thermal Conductivity", "description": "The relation (Nanoparticles, Thermal Conductivity) describes the role of nanoparticles in enhancing the thermal conductivity of phase change materials (PCMs). Enhancing the thermal conductivity of phase change material is the primary purpose for adding nanoparticles, as PCMs suffer from an inherent low thermal conductivity limitation that nanoparticles are specifically used to overcome. Both the addition and integration of nanoparticles into phase change materials, as well as their use as additives, are consistently noted to significantly influence and increase the thermal conductivity of PCMs.

The effectiveness of nanoparticles in this relation is attributed to their intrinsic properties. Nanoparticles possess a high surface-to-volume ratio and enhanced thermal conductivity, characteristics that improve PCM heat transfer rates. In practical application, dispersing nanoparticles in paraffin wax has been shown to increase the thermal conductivity of the PCM, resulting in greater heat transfer.

