Analysis: This table is the comparative literature review table explicitly referenced as "Table 1" in Section 4.1 (Discussion) of the surrounding document, complemented by the graph in Fig 7. It is organized as a 7-column matrix where each row represents one research study, with columns capturing: (1) lead researcher names (Wang et al., Aslfattahi et al., Iasiello et al., Bose et al., Abdeali and Liu et al., Yuandong et al.), (2) publication year, (3) research focus, (4) enhancement technique/material, (5) quantitative performance outcome, (6) application domain, and (7) stated significance.

**Structure and data points:** The table spans six studies published between 2020 and 2022 (two in 2020, three in 2021, one in 2022), indicating this is a rapidly evolving research area. The studies cluster into four enhancement strategies: (a) nanocomposite doping — Aslfattahi et al. (2020) achieved 0.45 W/m·K conductivity with organic PCM/MXene nanocomposites, a 10x improvement over the base material, the most dramatic result in the table; (b) porous metal foam integration — Iasiello et al. (2021) reduced PCM melting time by 30% using metal foam/PCM structures for building energy efficiency, and Bose et al. (2021) increased heat transfer efficiency by 18% using micro-pore metal foams for solar collectors and thermoelectric systems; (c) nanostructure stabilization — Abdeali and Liu et al. (2020) achieved thermal storage stability above 95% with nanostructure-supported shape-stabilized PCMs; and (d) additive manufacturing — Yuandong et al. (2021) improved thermal conductivity by 20% using 3D-printed lattice heat sink structures with PCM for electronics and aerospace.

**Statistical insights:** Excluding the MXene outlier, conventional enhancement techniques deliver a narrow improvement band of 15–20% (Wang: 15% conductivity gain; Bose: 18% efficiency; Yuandong: 20% conductivity), averaging roughly 17.7%. This clustering suggests a practical ceiling for incremental approaches, while the MXene nanocomposite result (10x) demonstrates that nanomaterial selection can produce step-change improvements — consistent with the surrounding text's emphasis on thermal conductivity as the dominant challenge in nanofluid/PCM characterization.

**Relationship to surrounding context:** The table directly substantiates three themes from the document. First, it supports the Section 4.1 claim that thermal conductivity is the critical property under investigation, as five of six rows report conductivity or heat-transfer metrics. Second, it validates the Section V discussion of "PCM using Porous Medium for Electronics Cooling in Aerospace," which highlights metal foams and graphite matrices as lightweight, high-surface-area heat-transfer enhancers — precisely the mechanism quantified by Iasiello et al. (30% melting time reduction) and Bose et al. (18% efficiency gain). Third, the aerospace thread is reinforced by Wang et al. (spacecraft thermal energy storage, 15% conductivity enhancement for spacecraft systems) and Yuandong et al. (electronics and aerospace, +20% conductivity), connecting the comparative data to the paper's stated aerospace electronics-cooling focus. The breadth of applications — spacecraft, renewable energy storage, building efficiency, solar collectors, thermoelectric, and general energy storage systems — illustrates the cross-domain relevance of PCM enhancement, echoing the surrounding text's observation that diverse material combinations (metal carbides, oxides, nanoparticles) are being explored across fluids, oils, and base matrices."}
