{"entity": "Thermal Management", "type": "method", "description": "Thermal Management is the process of controlling, regulating, and maintaining the temperature of a system to ensure optimal operating conditions. It constitutes a critical engineering concern across all phases of the energy delivery process—generation, conversion, and storage—since excessive heat reduces both performance and lifespan; improper heat regulation causes nearly 50% of electronic device malfunctions. As an academic field, Thermal Management is a prominent domain of scientific discourse, appearing in publications, reviews, and book chapters, spanning heat transfer enhancement, electronic cooling, and passive cooling strategies. It appears as a research topic within the soft matter physics community (cond-mat.soft classification), serves as the contextual framework for National Science Foundation-funded research programs, and is closely associated with thermal energy storage research.\n\nThermal Management relies heavily on Phase Change Materials (PCMs) as a central enabling technology, encompassing nano-enhanced PCMs (NePCM), nanoencapsulated PCMs, nanoconfinement techniques, solid-solid PCMs, and composite PCMs. PCMs utilize latent heat for temperature regulation, while encapsulation addresses material leakage concerns. Additional strategies include fins-enhanced systems, pin fin and plate-fin heat sinks, lattice heatsink/PCM systems, porous metal foams, specialized coatings, air cooling, and liquid cooling, along with coordinating heat dissipation and heat-conducting materials via convection and conduction. Thermal conductivity enhancement—including paraffin wax improvement and thermally conductive nanofillers—is a key research topic, and advanced analyses incorporate entropy generation and magnetohydrodynamic effects for electronics-cooling applications.\n\nIn electronics and aerospace contexts, Thermal Management denotes managing operating temperatures of electronic components up to their highest allowable limits, preventing overheating in extreme environments, and ensuring device reliability, motivating passive technologies such as PCM heat sinks that absorb and store heat; PCM melting dynamics remain an active research area as of 2024. For lithium-ion batteries, it represents the methodology for controlling cell temperature within an optimum range via PCM pouches, encapsulation, air cooling, and liquid cooling, extending to heat dissipation control in battery packs of light electric vehicles. In energy generation, it mitigates temperature-related losses in photovoltaic systems—standard PV panels, photovoltaic thermal systems, concentrator photovoltaic systems, and Building Integrated Concentrating Photovoltaic (BICPV) systems—yielding measurable gains in output current, electrical efficiency, and power output, and it encompasses thermal behavior control in solar collectors, including photovoltaic cooling via porous media. In building contexts, it is the target of additively manufactured, gel-stabilized paraffin composites for dynamic heat management. Applications further span architectural buildings, glazed windows, food packaging, power electronics, and UAV payloads.\n\nOverall, Thermal Management stands as a core engineering domain and research theme concerning heat dissipation mechanisms, cooling systems, thermal insulation (including silica-based insulation and heat control in nanostructured materials), and the thermal regulation applications of phase-change materials.<SEP>Practice guided by the model to mitigate heating effects in hybrid/external-cavity semiconductor lasers."}
