{"entity1": "Phase Change Materials", "entity2": "Thermal Energy Storage", "description": "The relation (Phase Change Materials, Thermal Energy Storage) captures the fundamental role of Phase Change Materials (PCMs) as both a medium and a technology for thermal energy storage, for which they are widely recognized as a remarkable medium and repeatedly applied, developed, and researched. The core mechanism underlying this relation is latent heat storage: PCMs absorb thermal energy at certain temperatures during phase transitions—typically liquid-solid transitions involving melting and freezing—and gradually release it as heat is charged and discharged in response to temperature variations. Material variants include organic PCMs, nano-enhanced PCMs, microencapsulated PCMs, and biomimetic and bio-derived composites. A key limitation is that the low thermal conductivity of PCMs restricts heat transfer rates in thermal energy storage applications; performance improvements have therefore been achieved through nanoparticle dispersion, which enhances thermal conductivity and heat transfer. A related phenomenon involves pressure-induced changes in PCMs that affect the phase transition temperature, enabling simultaneous thermal energy storage and distribution against the temperature gradient.\n\nA significant dimension of this relation concerns construction and building applications. PCMs provide the mechanism for storing and releasing thermal energy in construction materials: they are incorporated into concrete, integrated into cement-lime mortars, impregnated into mortars, and embedded in composite materials—such as the enhanced composite studied in a 2019 paper—thereby endowing these building materials with thermal energy storage capability and improving energy efficiency. In building envelopes and smart building materials, PCM-based thermal energy storage systems function as passive solutions for reducing peak heat loads, stabilizing indoor temperatures, and passively regulating building temperatures, while also enabling heat reuse in the built environment.\n\nBeyond buildings, PCMs are applied in thermal energy storage systems for solar energy storage, building thermal management and climate control, electronics cooling, aerospace contexts (where they absorb thermal energy), and heat storage technologies, and they supply the thermal energy storage function in hybrid heat sink designs. Thermal energy storage using PCMs is regarded as an important solution to the growing energy crisis and environmental problems, with energy-related applications such as heat transfer enhancement, within which specific studies—including research on PCM fin-melting behavior—are situated.\n\nFinally, PCMs and thermal energy storage frequently appear together as a shared topic in academic literature, where numerous review articles and papers identify thermal energy storage as their core subject and treat phase change materials as the central technology, with conclusions affirming that PCMs play an increasingly important role in thermal energy storage and building technologies. Their significance further extends to knowledge retrieval and application within the thermal energy storage domain."}
