{"entity1": "Multi-PCM Lime Mortars", "entity2": "Phase Change Materials", "description": "The relation (Multi-PCM Lime Mortars, Phase Change Materials) describes the incorporation of phase change materials (PCMs) as integral components within multi-PCM lime mortars. These mortars integrate multiple PCMs—typically two simultaneously—into the same lime matrix, with each pair combined at a total dosage of 20% by weight of lime (bwol). The incorporated PCMs are characterized by different phase transition temperatures and typologies, and they may be introduced either as raw materials or in processed forms.\n\nThe PCMs are incorporated in three distinct formats: form-stable (FS), powder microencapsulated, and slurry microencapsulated forms, with the latter two constituting the microencapsulated (MS, MP) formats. This combination of formats and melting points yields several material variants—namely FS5, MS18, MP18, FS25, MS25, and MP25—whose numeric designations reflect the different melting points of the constituent PCMs.\n\nThe purpose of incorporating multiple PCMs with different melting points into multi-PCM lime mortars is to expand the thermal operating range of the mortars and of building materials more broadly. By combining PCMs with staggered phase transition temperatures, these mortars achieve extended latent heat storage ranges, enabling effective thermal regulation in buildings. Importantly, this multi-PCM integration is accomplished without compromising the load-bearing capacity of the lime matrix, thereby preserving the structural performance of the mortar while simultaneously enhancing its thermal energy storage and temperature-regulating capabilities."}
