{"entity1": "Latent Heat Storage", "entity2": "Phase Change Materials", "description": "The relation (Latent Heat Storage, Phase Change Materials) denotes the fundamental connection between latent heat storage technology and phase change materials (PCMs), in which PCMs serve as the essential enabling medium through which latent heat storage is realized. Latent heat storage is a thermal energy storage approach that operates by utilizing the latent heat absorbed or released when a phase change material undergoes a phase transition. PCMs store and release energy via liquid-solid phase transitions—such as melting and freezing—and latent heat storage relies fundamentally on this phase change behavior to store heat.\n\nA defining characteristic of the relation is that phase change materials possess high latent heat storage properties, which makes them particularly valuable for thermal energy storage. PCMs enable latent heat storage over their phase transition temperature intervals and permit storage to occur at nearly constant temperatures, a feature that distinguishes this storage approach and underpins its practical utility. When embedded within other systems or materials, PCMs likewise provide latent heat storage and release during their phase transitions.\n\nIn application terms, phase change materials function as latent heat storage media, enabling latent heat thermal energy storage for purposes such as thermal regulation and cooling-load reduction. In building and construction contexts, for example, the latent-heat storage contribution of PCMs can be quantified by comparing the effusivity of reference mortars with that of PCM-impregnated mortars.\n\nThe relation is also well established within academic and research contexts: latent heat storage is an area of active interest for researchers studying phase change materials, and scholarly literature frequently discusses PCMs in terms of their latent heat storage properties, their melting and freezing conditions, and the fundamental mechanisms by which latent heat storage is achieved. Across such discussions, phase change materials are consistently identified as the materials utilized for latent heat storage applications.<SEP>Phase change materials are used for latent heat storage, a core mechanism discussed in the review article.<SEP>Phase Change Materials are associated with latent heat storage technologies.<SEP>Phase change materials store and release energy via latent heat during phase change, underpinning latent heat storage.<SEP>Phase change materials provide latent heat storage capacity examined in PCM-based cooling applications."}
