{"entity1": "Latent Heat Thermal Energy Storage", "entity2": "Phase Change Materials", "description": "The relation between Latent Heat Thermal Energy Storage (LHTES) and Phase Change Materials (PCMs) describes the fundamental role of PCMs as the core storage medium enabling LHTES, which constitutes a novel method of thermal energy storage. Within this relationship, LHTES systems store thermal energy by exploiting the phase transition of PCMs—particularly their melting behavior—whereby the materials absorb heat during phase change and subsequently release it, thereby accomplishing both the storage and discharge of thermal energy.\n\nPCMs have been widely investigated within LHTES systems for thermal management applications, and certain latent heat thermal storage systems employ multiple PCMs to store heat. The relation also extends to practical building applications: PCMs incorporated in mortars provide LHTES capability, and academic research has experimentally validated the heat storage and release performance of PCMs in roofing systems.\n\nFrom a research perspective, this relation encompasses key scientific concerns, notably the stability and characterization of PCMs, which are central issues in LHTES research, as well as various enhancement strategies targeting the LHTES applications served by PCMs.<SEP>Phase change materials serve as the storage medium for latent heat thermal energy storage systems.<SEP>Phase Change Materials enable latent heat thermal energy storage behavior observed in the study's comparative experiments."}
