{"entity": "Latent Heat Storage", "type": "concept", "description": "Latent Heat Storage is a form of thermal energy storage that stores and releases thermal energy by exploiting the latent heat absorbed or released during phase transitions of phase change materials (PCMs), typically between solid and liquid states over defined temperature intervals. As the storage category most closely associated with PCMs—and enabled by their high heat capacity, typically absorbing 150–250 kJ/kg during melting—it is also regarded as a broader energy conservation approach. The capacity of a PCM unit is characterized by C_pcm = m_pcm·L_f together with its melting temperature T_m. Compared with sensible heat storage, it is generally preferred for its higher energy density per unit volume or mass, its ability to store a greater concentration of heat at a nearly constant temperature, and the potential for more efficient, smaller, simpler, more practical, and less costly systems.

Its efficiency can be enhanced through heat transfer augmentation, such as bridging the gap between a heat source and the PCM using copper fins; extended surfaces or fins within the PCM domain and nanomaterial additives significantly influence PCM thermal conductivity. Within nanotechnology, it is the underlying thermal management mechanism exploited by nano-PCMs, optimizable through consistent particle dimensions of synthesized nanocapsules, while composite PCMs based on porous silica nanomaterials have also been developed; review literature notes that nano-modified PCM performance still requires further experimental validation, particularly regarding melting/freezing conditions and charge–discharge processes.

In construction materials, Latent Heat Storage is the primary thermal contribution mechanism of PCMs—rather than altered conductive heat transfer—providing latent heat capacity and thermal buffering in lime-based renders and mortars, where heat is stored and released during complete melting and crystallization of low- and high-transition PCMs; multi-PCM mortar systems extend storage ranges. Its contribution can be quantified by comparing the effusivity of reference and PCM-impregnated mortars, and when fully utilized it directly increases the thermal inertia of mortars, as shown with microencapsulated paraffin waxes. The soft Fischer–Tropsch wax supplies this mechanism in studied composites, and it relates to technology claims concerning thermal insulation materials and PCM encapsulation. In building envelopes, performance is assessed through four key indicators—HGR, peak heat flux reduction (PR), temperature reduction (ΔT), and time-lag difference (Δτ)—with ventilation possibly serving as the discharge mechanism. Paraffin PCMs provide storage through phase transition near 8 °C, calcium chloride hexahydrate offers high capacity valuable in building applications, and PCMs leveraging this mechanism moderate indoor temperatures without active mechanical systems—a technology domain central to building energy efficiency research, supported by references such as Raoux in construction review literature.

As an operating strategy, latent heat storage may be relied upon solely (PCM-only mode) or used concurrently with active heat rejection (parallel mode), enabling temperature-peak clipping and strategic heat routing. Beyond buildings, it improves electronics cooling—justifying abrupt temperature rise and enhancing the thermal stability of PCM-based heat sinks under free convection—and positions PCMs as storage media for cooling applications. It improves the thermal performance and efficiency of domestic appliances, serves industrial purposes including the storage and reuse of otherwise-lost process heat, and combines with photothermal energy conversion in advanced composites, enabling continuous operation beyond daylight hours. As an active research topic frequently examined in papers on PCM fundamentals, reviews, and recommendations—including the "Thermal Energy Storage" reference literature—latent heat storage provides an efficient and fundamental mechanism for temperature control and overall thermal management.<SEP>Thermal storage function provided by the Phase Change Material layer beneath the saline water in the PCM condition.<SEP>The approximately 21.85 kJ of latent heat storage capacity obtained by multiplying the beeswax latent heat of 242.8191 kJ/kg by the 0.09 kg of beeswax used.<SEP>Energy storage mechanism based on phase change latent heat; its characterization is cited as a methodological contribution of the 2020 review.<SEP>Heat storage capacity of phase change materials, examined as part of validating PCM-heat sink performance in practical applications.<SEP>A thermal energy storage mechanism based on absorbing and releasing heat during phase transitions of materials such as phase change materials."}
