{"entity": "Latent Heat", "type": "concept", "description": "Latent heat is the thermal energy absorbed or released by a substance during a phase change—such as melting, solidification, or crystallization—while the material remains at a constant or nearly constant temperature, in contrast to sensible heat, which manifests as a temperature change; it is significantly greater than the heat accompanying ordinary temperature changes without phase transition. In thermodynamic modeling, the latent heat (Q_l) of phase transition is governed by the energy equation and commonly expressed as ΔH = βL, where β is the liquid fraction and L the latent heat of fusion, representing the maximum latent heat content attained at complete melting.

Latent heat is the fundamental mechanism by which phase change materials (PCMs) store thermal energy—rather than through sensible heat—forming a storage approach in which PCMs charge and discharge heat driven by temperature variations. During melting, excess heat is absorbed nearly isothermally; during solidification, the stored heat is released, as when paraffin wax discharges heat nearly isothermally, keeping a PCM/copper wall near-constant in temperature. This isothermal storage reduces heat losses, buffers heat flux across building envelopes, delays heat-sink temperature rise, and alleviates temperature fluctuations, producing stabilization plateaus in temperature-versus-time curves, increasing thermal inertia, and stabilizing temperatures in mortars, cementitious composites, construction materials, soils, PCM glazing, and indoor environments. The latent energy of PCMs is estimated to average 2.5–3 times that of water; heat-storage capacity scales with PCM mass fraction times latent heat, plateau duration with absorbed PCM mass, and latent heat enters cooling-load calculations, scaling linearly for PCM while remaining fixed for substances such as ice.

Quantitatively, latent heat L is a baseline thermophysical property of pure PCMs and a covered property of nano-enhanced PCMs, obtained via low-temperature differential scanning calorimetry (LT-DSC) from the areas of bell-shaped phase-change peaks—roughly 200 kJ/kg (191.29–209.52 kJ/kg) for three studied PCMs—and measurable by the T-history method. Together with effective heat capacity and the phase-change temperature range, it determines phase-change behavior, underlying the thermal buffering captured by the parameter Q* through absorption during heating ramps and release during crystallization on cooling ramps.

Illustrative applications include a 23 °C melting-point PCM markedly increasing thermal storage capacity; OM42 absorbing latent heat earlier than OM55 owing to its lower 42 °C melting point; RT27 absorbing heat within its 24–29 °C range while maintaining front-surface temperature between 50 and 200 minutes; heat penetrating an aluminum plate initiating melting near 35 °C; and stored latent heat discharged during 9:00–11:00, yielding a genuine morning heat-release advantage.

Nevertheless, latent heat is confined to a narrow temperature interval and cannot solely account for overall ΔT increases across the full temperature span; single-PCM systems may underutilize latent-heat storage potential, whereas multi-PCM systems improve utilization. It also delays heat recovery, preventing real-time energy transfer after noon, and its utilization degrades after mid-June when elevated night-time temperatures cause incomplete solidification. Gel-form PCMs exhibit favorable transient latent-heat effects despite failing under steady-state conditions, and in PCM-based thermal barriers, latent-heat utilization efficiency is governed by additively manufactured geometry.<SEP>Thermal energy stored by Phase Change Material during peak solar radiation and released after radiation declines, enabling sustained freshwater production in the PCM-equipped system.<SEP>The heat released by Phase Change Material that sustains distillation productivity longer into afternoon and evening hours.<SEP>Heat stored by the Phase Change Material during phase change and released gradually in afternoon/evening hours, extending production duration of the solar distillation system.<SEP>Heat stored by PCM during daytime phase change and released gradually in the afternoon and at night, sustaining higher water temperatures (8.3% higher at 15.00) and prolonging distillate production.<SEP>The form in which absorbed energy is stored within the material after the PEG chains undergo a phase transition during the energy storage step.<SEP>The heat energy mechanism utilized by Phase Change Materials for temperature regulation during melting and solidifying state transitions."}
{"entity": "Phase Change Materials", "type": "artifact", "description": ""Phase Change Materials" (PCMs) refers to two distinct classes of materials that share the same name: (1) thermal energy storage materials used in thermal management engineering, and (2) chalcogenide semiconductors used in electronic, photonic, and data storage applications.

In thermal engineering, Phase Change Materials are substances with a high latent heat of fusion that melt and solidify within a narrow temperature range while storing and releasing substantial amounts of heat. Examples include paraffin waxes, whose melting behavior and phase change characteristics underpin their thermal energy storage capability. These materials are employed for passive thermal management of batteries in electric vehicles, regulating battery thermal behavior and extending heat holding capacity. Implementation approaches include PCM encapsulation pouches, pure encapsulation designs, hybrid liquid cooling configurations, and combinations with heat pipe technology for hybrid thermal management of lithium-ion batteries, with passive approaches studied in contrast to active and hybrid cooling methods. PCMs are also used as passive cooling solutions for photovoltaic systems, absorbing heat and providing temperature drops without requiring energy input, and they find broader application in solar PV cooling, battery management, and green buildings.

In materials science and electronics, Phase Change Materials are a special class of semiconductor materials, most commonly composed of chalcogenide alloys such as GST-124 and GST-225, that can be reversibly switched between amorphous and crystalline phases. These thermally activated transitions are typically induced by laser pulses or controlled thermal pulses. Their exceptional optical and electrical contrast between phases makes them valuable for encoding and rewriting information in data storage, memory devices, and computing applications, as well as in photonics and nanophotonics—including optical switching, the subject of dedicated theses and reports—where their optical properties can be locally modified. PCMs undergo crystallization involving nucleation and crystal growth, and are sub-classified as nucleation-dominated or growth-dominated based on their dominant crystallization mechanism. Crystallization kinetics, cyclability, and the asymmetric crystallization window are central to their switching dynamics. Metavalent bonding plays a central role in this class, and telluride-based compounds exhibit structures amenable to fast phase transitions. These materials are studied using first-principles methods, through which new ternary compositions are predicted from binary parent compounds; additionally, confining heat within the material enhances thermal efficiency and reduces the energy required for phase transition.

"Phase Change Materials" is also the title of a review article by Simone Raoux, published as a book by Springer US in 2009, and cited in the literature (e.g., as reference 28 in source documents)."}
{"entity": "Latent Heat Of Fusion", "type": "concept", "description": "The latent heat of fusion is a thermophysical property that quantifies the amount of thermal energy a material absorbs per unit mass during the solid-liquid phase transition, thereby stabilizing temperature. Functionally, it operates as the heat absorption mechanism through which a phase change material (PCM) takes in thermal energy during melting, delaying the temperature rise of the associated heat sink. This property is leveraged by PCMs to absorb transient peak thermal loads while maintaining PV modules in an isothermal state.

As a material property, the latent heat of fusion, commonly denoted L or L_f with units of J/kg, quantifies a material's heat storage capacity during the solid-liquid transition. When multiplied by the PCM mass (m_pcm), it defines the PCM's energy storage capacity, yielding the total latent heat storage capacity C_pcm. Reported values vary across materials and measurements: 184.48 kJ/kg for one specific PCM, 110 kJ/kg for another obtained by integrating the melting peak, 190–220 kJ/kg for OM 42, 200–230 kJ/kg for OM 55, 200–280 kJ/kg for salt hydrates, and 180–210 kJ/kg for fatty acids, as compiled in tabulated references.

Experimental determination is typically carried out through differential scanning calorimetry (DSC), where the latent heat of fusion corresponds to the endothermic enthalpy associated with PCM melting observed during the heating cycle of the measurement.

In enthalpy-based modeling, L_f acts as a material property that scales the liquid fraction β to yield the latent heat term ΔH = βL within the total enthalpy decomposition of the PCM. The quantity L_f also enters the melting term and the definitions of the local, solid-phase, and liquid-phase enthalpies. The coefficient L_f/C_p carries dimensions of temperature (K), representing the temperature equivalent of the latent heat. In numerical simulations, L_f is fixed at 10³ in lattice units and appears in the Stefan number St = C_p·ΔT/L_f, contributing to a sensible-to-latent heat ratio fixed at St = 10."}
