{"reference_id": "6", "content": "demonstrated enhanced temperature stabilization, with maximum ∆T values of 29.55 K (30% PCM) and 28.61 K (honeycomb 30%). These results confirm that the geometry pro duced by additive manufacturing plays a decisive role in governing heat transfer and latent heat utilization in PCM-based thermal barriers.

Keywords: phase change materials (PCMs); 3D printing; additive manufacturing; heat management

1. Introduction

Improving the thermal properties of building partitions and cold stores is one of the key engineering challenges in terms of enhancing energy efficiency and reducing the carbon footprint of the construction and refrigeration sectors. Thermal energy storage (TES) systems, particularly those based on phase change materials (PCMs), are increasingly being analyzed as passive solutions for reducing peak heat loads and stabilizing indoor temperatures [1–3]. PCMs can store and release significant amounts of energy within a narrow temperature range thanks to their reversible solid–liquid phase transition, which is characterized by a high enthalpy of transition (see Figure 1) [1,4]. When incorporated into the components or walls of a refrigeration facility, they can minimize temperature fluctuations and redistribute thermal loads over time. This reduces the amount of energy required for heating and cooling [2,5,6].

Despite these advantages, wider use of PCM in construction is hindered by two significant technical limitations: the risk of leakage in the liquid phase and the low thermal conductivity of many phase change materials. The latter restricts the rate at which heat is exchanged and reduces the efficiency with which latent heat is utilized in dynamic conditions [1,3,7]. To improve heat transfer, the use of conductive additives such as graphite and carbon fillers, as well as metallic foams and hybrid composites, was proposed [3,8,9]. However, improving thermal conductivity usually means reducing the proportion of the active phase of the PCM, which reduces energy storage capacity.

Meanwhile, advances in additive manufacturing technology have enabled the design of materials with specific geometric structures, porosity levels, and heat conduction paths [10–14]. Research indicates that the geometry of 3D-printed cellular structures significantly impacts thermal resistance and heat flux distribution within partitions [12–14]. This means that both the material composition and the structure topology are key design parameters in PCM systems. Previous research on integrating PCMs with additive tech nologies can be divided into three main strategies. The first strategy involves introducing microencapsulated phase-change material (MEPCM) directly into printable mineral mix tures, such as mortars or concretes [15–17]. Studies have shown that this solution reduces thermal conductivity and enables materials to accumulate latent heat [15,16]. However, increased PCM content significantly impacts the rheology of the fresh mixture, which may hinder printability [17]. The second strategy is to activate the PCM selectively by placing it in a specific zone of the element. This optimizes the thermal response without changing the entire cross-section of the partition [18]. The third approach uses macroencapsulation, which involves using a 3D printer to design internal cavities or channels and then filling them with PCM [19–21]. Studies have shown that cavity geometry affects phase transition dynamics, extreme temperature attenuation, and energy efficiency [20,21]. Despite numerous studies confirming the validity of integrating PCM with additive technologies, a significant research gap remains in the literature. Most studies focus on either modifying the material (reducing λ in MEPCM composites) or analyzing the dynamic response of macroencapsulated panels.

Table 1 provides an overview of PCM integration strategies in additive manufacturing. However, comparative studies that systematically analyze the influence of cell structure topology and PCM integration methods on steady-state parameters (λ, R) and transient response (∆T) under identical boundary conditions are rarely conducted. Table 2, on the other hand, provides an overview of experimental studies on different types of PCM oils in various applications, not just additive manufacturing. The collected works demonstrate that selecting different types of PCM oils (e.g., paraffin, fatty acids, esters, and bio-oils) and considering their rheological state (e.g., liquid versus solidified) can alter the “effective” activation temperature and modify the heat transfer dynamics observed in q(t) and q(T), even when the nominal $\mathrm { T } _ { \mathrm { m } }$ is similar.

However, more research is needed to better understand the influence of architectural geometry on heat exchange at the solid-PCM phase boundary and the role of PCM distribution within printed cellular structures on the macroscopic thermal resistance of partitions. Therefore, future research must develop design tools that consider the following three factors simultaneously: (1) PCM selection (Tm, ∆H, and cycle stability); (2) carrier material properties (k, strength, and compatibility); and (3) geometry (PCM fraction, cavity shape, PCM-matrix contact area, and conduction lengths). It is particularly important to standardize the methods of assessing the “insulation-accumulation” function of the partition, as many publications report conductivity (k) and dynamic parameters (∆T, time lag, and DF), but less often provide the U-value. The U-value requires full characterization of the partition under boundary conditions.

This paper analyzes the thermal behavior of PCM-containing partitions manufactured using additive manufacturing in two different cell geometries: Kelvin cell structures and honeycomb structures. Two PCM integration strategies were considered: directly introducing PCM into a photopolymerizable resin and filling the cell volume with a PCMbased gel after printing. This study aimed to explain how the structure’s topology and PCM introduction method affect its thermal response, including heat transfer, temperature stabilization, and overall energy storage and release efficiency during heating. These results may contribute to the rational design of PCM-based architectural heat management elements for construction and engineering applications. Due to their thermal properties, thermal partitions containing PCM are an innovative construction solution. Using them can reduce electricity consumption and increase the energy"}
