Analysis: This is Figure 3 from a research letter on heat transfer in Phase Change Materials (PCMs) with multiple fin insertion, specifically presenting a two-panel analysis of early-stage melting dynamics in the purely conductive regime (Rayleigh number Ra = 0, cf. eq. (5)). Panel (a) plots the normalized liquid fraction ⟨φ⟩ (cfr. eq. (7)) as a function of the dimensionless time t⋆ = t/t_max for multiple geometrical fin configurations (finless, finned configurations i–v including 'rect' and 'square', and plated configurations 'lowplate'/'midplate', illustrated in fig. 1) at two Stefan numbers St = 1 and St = 10 (obtained via Cp = 10³ and 10⁴). The key finding shown: a lower Stefan number delivers slower melting, but the separation between the 'square' configuration (fins further apart) and all other configurations occurs regardless of St, merely delayed in time—demonstrating the robustness of the trend. Plated configurations consistently perform worse. Panel (b) presents 3D snapshots of the local liquid fraction field φ(x⃗) at St = 10 for the 'rect' and 'square' configurations at two time instants: t⋆ = 1.5·10⁻² (when square visibly separates from other configurations) and t⋆ = 1.0·10⁻¹ (the final time in panel (a)), with each plot including a front-facing perspective view at the bottom right. A third row adds the 'lowplate' configuration, providing visual proof of its worse spatial efficiency. The snapshots reveal that interference/overlap in the molten zones already appears for the 'rect' configuration at t⋆ = 1.5·10⁻² while being absent for 'square' at the same time; this overlap wastes thermal energy on already-melted substance and is responsible for the reduced total molten volume in closely spaced fin arrangements. The figure directly supports the paper's central hypothesis that the relative distance between fins matters—further-apart fins gain an advantage that cascades into overall better melting performance. This figure was designed to isolate geometric effects from convective effects by examining the conductive regime, complementing Figure 2's convection-dominated results (Ra = 2.0·10² to 1.2·10⁶)."}
