{"entity1": "Phase Change Material", "entity2": "Thermal Conductivity", "description": "The relation between Phase Change Material (PCM) and Thermal Conductivity characterizes how the intrinsic thermal conductivity of PCMs influences their performance in thermal energy storage applications, as well as their effect on the thermal properties of composite materials into which they are incorporated.

A fundamental and widely recognized characteristic of phase change materials, particularly pure and organic PCMs, is their relatively low thermal conductivity. This low thermal conductivity is considered a key limitation of PCM technology, as it restricts heat transfer performance and results in a slow distribution of stored heat throughout the material. This drawback is especially pronounced in organic PCMs and pure phase change materials.

When PCMs are incorporated into host matrices, they exert a remarkable effect on thermal properties by reducing the overall thermal conductivity of the resulting composite. For instance, the addition of PCM to cement-lime pastes was found to reduce their thermal conductivity, with the reduction being especially significant at temperatures above the PCM melting point. This effect is dose-dependent, as demonstrated by the observation that the lowest thermal conductivity values were obtained for samples containing 30% PCM.

To mitigate the limitation of low intrinsic thermal conductivity, enhancement approaches such as nanoparticle dispersion have been investigated. In this context, thermal conductivity plays a critical yet non-deterministic role in PCM performance, with its value increasing nonlinearly as nanoparticle concentration rises. This nonlinear behavior suggests that while improving thermal conductivity is an important consideration in PCM optimization, it is not the sole determinant of overall performance, and other factors also influence the effectiveness of phase change materials in practical applications.<SEP>Thermal conductivity is identified as the critical property and dominant challenge in PCM characterization, reported by five of six comparative studies."}
