{"entity1": "Latent Heat", "entity2": "Phase Change Material", "description": "The Latent Heat-Phase Change Material relation describes the fundamental thermal mechanism by which phase change materials (PCMs) interact with latent heat—thermal energy absorbed or released during phase transitions between solid and liquid states. When a PCM melts, it absorbs latent heat from its surroundings during the phase transition; conversely, when it solidifies, the stored latent heat is released back. Through this mechanism, PCMs absorb excess heat and store it as latent heat, enabling effective management of thermal loads in their environment.

A key characteristic of this relation is that latent heat exchange occurs while the material maintains a nearly constant temperature, allowing PCMs to provide temperature stabilization of components such as heat sinks. This temperature-regulating property underlies the value of PCMs in passive thermal management applications.

The latent heat behavior also manifests measurably in PCM-based materials. For example, pastes containing PCM exhibit an inflection point corresponding to the PCM melting point at 23 °C, which directly relates to latent heat. Similarly, latent heat governs the thermal response of PCMs as they transition between solid and liquid states near 35 °C, indicating that different PCM formulations exhibit latent heat effects at their characteristic transition temperatures.

However, the latent heat storage properties of PCMs can introduce practical limitations: because the material stores rather than immediately transfers absorbed heat, latent heat storage caused a delay in heat recovery time, making the PCM disadvantageous for real-time heat recovery applications.<SEP>PCMs store heat through the latent heat of phase transition within a defined and narrow temperature range.<SEP>Phase change materials absorb and release latent heat during melting and solidification to regulate building temperatures.<SEP>Phase Change Material releases latent heat that sustains productivity into afternoon and evening hours."}
