Within comparative and analytical studies, paraffin appears as one of the PCM types evaluated and compared against other phase change materials. Furthermore, one paraffin variant is noted as being among the three PCMs that were thermally characterized prior to impregnation, indicating its use in porous substrate applications. Beyond its standalone use, paraffin also serves as a constituent of other phase change materials: certain PCMs possess paraffinic cores and phases consisting of paraffin, showing that paraffin can function as the core component within composite or encapsulated PCM structures.

Taken together, these descriptions consistently establish paraffin as a versatile and widely studied phase change material, valued in applications ranging from battery systems and heat transfer experiments to construction composites and encapsulated thermal storage materials.<SEP>Paraffin is a widely used phase change material (PCM) for latent heat thermal energy storage.<SEP>Paraffin serves as a phase change material demonstrating heat storage and release capabilities."}
