decreased to 0.036 W/(m·K) at 30% PCM, with R increasing to 0.590 m2·K/W. However,
at identical PCM content and thickness (20 mm), Kelvin structures consistently exhibited
lower λ and higher R than honeycomb structures, confirming the superior heat distribution
capability of the open-cell topology.
The present 20 mm specimens can be directly compared with previously published
additively manufactured partitions modified with glycerin [29], where the thermal con-
ductivity coefficient for single-layer samples with a thickness of 20 mm and a 5% glycerin
additive was 0.059 W/(m·K), and the thermal resistance was 0.315 (m2·K)/W. In the earlier
study, improvements in thermal resistance were attributed primarily to increased heat
capacity and modified conduction pathways within the cellular structure. For the current
Kelvin 20 mm structure with 30% paraffin PCM, R reached 0.697 m2·K/W, demonstrating
comparable or improved steady-state insulation performance relative to glycerin-modified
systems of identical thickness. For honeycomb structures, R = 0.590 m2·K/W, remaining
within a similar performance range.
The key distinction lies in the thermophysical mechanism: glycerin primarily enhances
sensible heat storage, whereas paraffin additionally provides latent heat storage near
8 ◦C. Nevertheless, under steady-state conditions, the structural topology and geometric
configuration remain the dominant factors governing λ and R.
4. Conclusions
Analysis of the temperature change ( ∆T) revealed that the geometry of the porous
structure and the proportion of PCM have a significant influence on the thermal response
of the tested samples. The results clearly demonstrate that the Kelvin geometry is more
thermally efficient than the classic cubic structure in the presence of PCM, particularly at
higher PCM contents (30% PCM in gel form).
This can be explained by analyzing the dominant heat transport mechanisms: heat
conduction in the solid matrix; sensible and latent heat accumulation in the PCM; and heat
exchange at the solid–PCM phase boundaries. Limited or suppressed microscale convective
effects also play a role.
The Kelvin structure is characterized by an isotropic pore network, a higher surface-
to-volume ratio, and a more uniform distribution of heat conduction paths. These features
promote intensified heat exchange between the matrix and PCM. This results in the phase
change enthalpy being utilized more efficiently, as evidenced by higher ∆T values. In
contrast, the honeycomb structure exhibits greater thermal anisotropy, whereby local
concentrations of solid material can lead to faster temperature increases without effective
thermal stabilization.
Thus, structural and geometric analysis is a valuable addition to traditional thermoan-
alytical methods, providing more comprehensive insights into the behavior of functional
materials. The analysis revealed that Kelvin-type porous geometry can greatly enhance the
thermal efficiency of PCM-containing materials and that the topology of the structure has a
