Analysis: Figure 3 presents a temperature profile graph depicting the discharging process of Phase Change Material (PCM) with a 1:1 (kg) ratio of PCM to fish inside a coolbox. The graph plots temperature (in °C) on the y-axis against time (in minutes) on the x-axis, with data recorded every 30 minutes over a 6-hour (360-minute) period. The chart visualizes the thermal behavior during the melting phase of the PCM at room temperature. According to surrounding text, this 1:1 ratio configuration exhibited a temperature range from -4.3°C to 7.1°C during the experiment. This figure is the first in a series of discharging process figures (Figures 3-6) that systematically compare different PCM-to-fish mass ratios (1:1, 2:1, 3:1, 4:1, and 0:1 control). The 1:1 ratio represents the lowest PCM proportion tested and shows a relatively rapid temperature rise compared to higher PCM ratios, demonstrating less effective thermal regulation. This chart supports the broader discussion by establishing a baseline for evaluating how increasing PCM quantities improve cooling performance and extend temperature stability duration in coolbox applications for fish preservation."}
{"reference_id": "9", "content": "2-D Numerical Analysis Of The Photovoltaic-Phase Change Material (PV-PCM) Model With Flat Fins

Muhammed Musab Bayat1,a,\*, Ertan Buyruk2,b, Mustafa Caner3,c

¹Sivas Cumhuriyet University, Electrical-Electronics Eng. Dept. Sivas, Türkiye

