Analysis: This chart (Fig. 8) is a dual-axis time-series plot presenting the daily variation of time lag (Δτ) between Case A (reference roof without PCM) and Case B (PCM-integrated roof), overlaid with the average temperature trend of the PCM-B-CRT27 core throughout the June monitoring period. The time lag, calculated via equation 5, represents the delay between peak external temperature and peak internal surface temperature, serving as a direct indicator of the PCM's heat damping/delaying capacity. Key elements include: (1) Δτ values exhibiting marked daily variability, generally ranging from 1 h 45 min to 17 h; (2) an anomalous reversal on June 4, where the internal peak temperature preceded the external peak; (3) the PCM core temperature curve progressing from below 27 °C in early June (indicating partial activation) to stabilizing around 30 °C, operating within or just beyond the latent phase transition range (referenced in Fig. 5). The chart reveals the critical relationship between PCM activation state and thermal lag performance: as core temperature rises beyond the phase change range of the RT27-based PCM, incomplete nighttime solidification occurs, progressively degrading latent heat exploitation. This visualization directly supports the paper's discussion on PCM thermal buffering effectiveness, the 23% monthly heat gain reduction, and the conclusion that PCM melting temperature selection must align with expected operative temperature ranges for optimal latent storage performance in climate-dependent applications."}
