Analysis: Table 2 is structured as a nine-column comparative literature matrix designed to enable systematic cross-study comparison of experimental research on phase change material (PCM) oils. The columns are: 'PCM Type/Oil', 'PCM Form', 'Tm Range [°C]', 'ΔH Range [kJ/kg]', 'Investigated Parameters', 'Experimental Methods', 'Application Domain', 'Key Findings Relevant to Dynamic Behavior', and 'Reference'. This organization allows readers to correlate material thermophysical properties (melting temperature, latent heat) with experimental characterization approaches and observed dynamic thermal behavior.

The visible data row documents the study by Kraiem et al. [22] on commercial paraffin blends (n-alkanes) in pure PCM form. The reported melting temperature range of 5–35°C spans exactly the band relevant to building comfort and passive thermal management, making paraffins suitable candidates for the thermal partitions discussed in the surrounding text. The latent heat of fusion range of 150–220 kJ/kg represents substantial energy storage density, with the upper value (220 kJ/kg) approaching the theoretical maximum for practical paraffinic PCMs.

The investigated parameters—Cp(T), k(T), ΔH(T), and transition width—were measured via DSC (differential scanning calorimetry) and temperature-resolved property analysis, establishing a methodological template for characterizing temperature-dependent thermal behavior. The application domain is Building TES (thermal energy storage), which directly connects to the paper's stated goal of reducing electricity consumption and increasing building energy efficiency through PCM-containing partitions.

The key finding—that broader molecular distributions in commercial paraffin blends lead to wider transition intervals and smoother q(T) curvature—is central to the surrounding text's argument. The context explicitly states that different PCM types 'can alter the effective activation temperature and modify the heat transfer dynamics observed in q(t) and q(T), even when the nominal Tm is similar.' The Kraiem et al. row provides concrete evidence for this claim: the smeared phase transition caused by mixed n-alkane chain lengths means the PCM does not activate at a single sharp temperature but gradually across the 5–35°C interval. This has direct implications for the paper's own experimental design, which measures transient response (∆T) and steady-state parameters (λ, R) in Kelvin cell and honeycomb structures.

Furthermore, this finding cautions that nominal Tm values reported in datasheets may not predict actual thermal regulation performance in printed cellular partitions—a critical consideration for the paper's proposed design framework, which must balance PCM selection (Tm, ΔH, cycle stability), carrier material properties, and geometry simultaneously. The table, spanning applications beyond additive manufacturing (unlike Table 1), broadens the evidence base for understanding PCM dynamic behavior across contexts."}
