Analysis: Table 3 presents a comprehensive compilation of thermophysical properties for 21 PCM types commonly used in PV-PCM cooling configurations, organized into seven columns: PCM Type, Melting Point (°C), Latent Heat (kJ/kg), Density (m³/kg), Thermal Conductivity (W/m·K), Specific Heat (kJ/kg), and Authors. The table distinguishes between solid (S-) and liquid (L-) phase properties where applicable, using row-spanning cells to group phase-specific data under each PCM type.

**PCM Categories and Distribution**: The table features three main PCM categories: (1) Organic PCMs including paraffin waxes (5 entries with melting points 5–72°C), fatty acids (capric acid, capric-palmitic acid), and commercial RT-series PCMs from Rubitherm (RT25, RT27, RT28, RT35, RT44); (2) Inorganic salt hydrates such as CaCl2.6H2O and its mixture with MgCl2.6H2O; and (3) Specialty PCMs including pork fat, n-docosane, polyethylene glycol, OM-29, and Waksol A. Paraffin wax is the most frequently listed PCM (appearing 5 times), confirming its dominance in PV cooling research.

**Melting Point Range**: Melting points span from 17.7°C (capric:palmitic acid by Browne et al.) to 72°C (paraffin wax Merck by Sardarabadi et al.), with the majority clustered between 25–45°C—ideal for PV cell cooling since typical operating temperatures range 40–60°C. The RT-series PCMs cover a gradient from 25°C (RT25) to 44°C (RT44), enabling selection based on specific climate conditions.

**Latent Heat Analysis**: Latent heat values range from 157 kJ/kg (RT35, Huang et al.) to 299 kJ/kg (OM-29, Rajvikram et al.). The highest-capacity PCMs include OM-29 (299 kJ/kg), PCM32/80 (280 kJ/kg), RT28 (245 kJ/kg), and n-docosane (249 kJ/kg). CaCl2.6H2O shows 191 kJ/kg, consistent with its selection by Singh et al. for PV panel cooling with aluminum containers.

**Density and Thermal Conductivity**: Inorganic PCMs demonstrate significantly higher density—CaCl2.6H2O (1560–1710 m³/kg) and PCM32/80 (1500 m³/kg)—compared to organic paraffins (typically 700–910 m³/kg). The CaCl2.6H2O-MgCl2.6H2O mixture exhibits exceptional thermal conductivity at 3.359 W/m·K, approximately 10–20 times higher than paraffin-based PCMs (0.14–0.33 W/m·K). This explains why Singh et al. and others selected salt hydrates despite their lower latent heat. PCM32/80 also shows enhanced conductivity at 0.4 W/m·K.

**Connection to Surrounding Context**: The table directly supports several studies discussed in the text: RT35 properties validate Al-Najjar and Mahdi's MATLAB model; RT25 and RT28 data correlate with Kibria et al.'s evaluation where RT28HC showed best performance but only 65% melting; CaCl2.6H2O data supports Singh et al.'s system that reduced panel temperatures by 18–23.3°C; PCM32/80 properties underpin Choubineh et al.'s fuzzy-logic study that achieved 10.3°C temperature reduction. The consistently low thermal conductivity of most PCMs (0.14–0.33 W/m·K) explains why multiple surrounding studies (Sharma et al., Abdelrazik et al.) explored enhancements like micro-fins, nanomaterials, and graphene nanoplatelets to improve heat transfer. The melting point range (17.7–72°C) aligns with the surrounding text's finding that PCM-based cooling reduces PV temperatures by up to 42%, as these melting points absorb heat precisely within the critical PV operating temperature band."}
