{"entity": "PV-PCM System", "type": "artifact", "description": "The PV-PCM System, or Photovoltaic-Phase Change Material system, is an integrated technological and thermal modeling framework that couples photovoltaic panels or solar cells with Phase Change Materials (PCM). The system's primary design objective is to provide advanced thermal regulation, manage waste heat, store excess thermal energy, and cool the solar cells to enhance overall photovoltaic efficiency. By utilizing the PCM's latent heat absorption capacity and phase transition behavior, alongside natural convection that dissipates heat from heated surfaces to ambient air, the system effectively moderates the panel's temperature profile.\n\nThrough this active thermal management, the PV-PCM System maintains the panel's temperature closer to the optimum operating temperature of 25°C. Validated research demonstrates that this integration successfully delays and reduces solar cell peak temperatures and peak times compared to standard reference systems. Consequently, the system achieves measurable efficiency improvements, including documented temperature reductions of up to 13.1°C.\n\nStructurally, the PV-PCM System features a multi-layered composition, frequently analyzed in cross-sectional views or simplified into a five-layer photovoltaic solar cell system with distinct front and back heat transfer surfaces. Key structural components typically include a top front glass cover, silicon solar cells, the phase change material layer itself, and a back aluminum container. To further optimize thermal regulation and heat dissipation, the system's design may incorporate internal fins. Researchers actively investigate various structural optimizations, such as testing different PCM thicknesses and proposing advanced fin geometries based on emerging analytical techniques.\n\nThe evaluation and analysis of the PV-PCM System rely heavily on accurate transient thermal simulations and comprehensive heat transfer methodologies. Researchers subject the integrated system to transient heat conduction problems, often simulating its thermal behavior over 24-hour periods to fully understand its capabilities. Computational models frequently utilize G_PV as the primary thermal input and apply specific boundary conditions to drive the analysis. Advanced evaluation techniques include 2D Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) analysis to assess the system's thermal performance. These validated thermal models are widely applied to analyze the system's effectiveness, including parametric studies designed to evaluate cooling efficiency in hot-climate regions such as Vietnam.<SEP>A system that structurally integrates a phase change material layer onto the rear backsheet of a photovoltaic module, creating an intelligent, self-actuating thermodynamic buffer that clamps module temperature during peak sun hours and resets via nighttime solidification.<SEP>An integrated photovoltaic and phase change material system for photovoltaic thermal management, achieving LCOE reductions up to 15%, EPBT of 1.5 to 5.1 years, GHG emission reductions of 10% to 30%, and hybrid configurations reaching 83% total efficiency.<SEP>Base hybrid system integrating phase change materials with photovoltaic modules; optimized PV-PCM systems offset initial investment through enhanced electricity yield and extended operational lifespan of cooled modules."}
