$ . Based on the core temperature of the PCM, the degree of PCM exploitation is estimated at $6 2 \\% ,$ calculated according to Equation 1.\n\nHowever, a clear change in behavior can be observed after June 12th, when the core temperature of the PCM consistently exceeds the upper limit of the latent phase range. From this date onward, the PCM remains predominantly in the liquid state during both daytime and nighttime, despite the activation of night ventilation. As a consequence, the material progressively loses its ability to undergo effective phase change cycles, leading to a reduced latent heat storage capacity. This behavior indicates that the selected PCM melting temperature (RT27) is underestimated with respect to the actual thermal loads and boundary conditions experienced during this period. The inability of the PCM to fully solidify overnight limits its thermal buffering potential and highlights the critical importance of selecting a PCM with a melting temperature appropriately matched to the expected operating temperature range.\n\nFigure 6 shows the daily trend of the incoming heat flow, comparing Case A (solid line) and Case B (dashed line). During the monitoring period, a marked difference between the two configurations can be observed. The reference configuration (Case A) shows more pronounced daily fluctuations, with larger peaks of heat gain and loss, while the configuration with PCM (Case B) shows a more attenuated thermal response. This behavior highlights the ability of PCM to store and release latent heat, reducing instantaneous variations in heat flow and improving the thermal stability of the building envelope.\n\nFurthermore, analysis of the heat flow peaks shows an average reduction of 55%, with daily values ranging from 27% to 90%. These results show that the integration of\n\nPCM not only helps to limit heat transfer through the building envelope, but also modifies the thermal dynamics of the system, attenuating daily fluctuations and reducing the intensity of peaks.\n\nOverall, the integration of PCM into the building system allows for a significant reduction in the transmitted heat load and an improvement in the dynamic behavior of the building envelope. The combined effect of latent heat storage and release delays and reduces thermal flow peaks, promoting more stable operating conditions and potentially reducing energy demand for cooling.\n\nThe total heat gain recorded in June is reduced from 1666 Wh/m² for Case A to 1286 Wh/m² for Case B, corresponding to a monthly reduction of 23%.\n\nIn addition, a significant reduction in internal surface temperature (Tsi) and operating temperature (Top) is observed in the PCM configuration (see Figure 7). The maximum reduction in Tsi reaches $4 . 5 ~ ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } ,$ highlighting the effective thermal attenuation capacity of PCM on the internal surface exposed to the environment, while the maximum increase, equal to $+ 0 . 5 \\mathrm { { ^ { \\circ } C } } ,$ is negligible, confirming that the activation of PCM does not cause discomfort during the heat release phases. Similarly, the operating temperature (Top) shows a maximum reduction of $1 . 8 ~ ^ { \\circ } \\bar { \\mathrm { C } }$ and a maximum increase $\\mathrm { 0 f + 0 . 7 ~ ^ { \\circ } C } .$ , indicating that PCM helps to limit heat accumulation even in the innermost layers of the system. These results confirm the effectiveness of PCM in reducing peak temperatures and stabilizing the thermal response of the building envelope, with positive effects on indoor comfort and reduction of the thermal load during the hottest hours.\n\nThe daily variation of Δτ calculated using equation 5 is shown in Figure 8 together with the average temperature trend of the core of the PCM-B-CRT27. The time lag indicates the delay between the peak external temperature and the peak internal surface temperature, providing a direct indicator of the PCM's ability"}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: This figure panel presents two complementary charts (Figure 3 and Figure 4) analyzing the cost-effectiveness of Phase Change Material (PCM) integration in building glazing systems for hot-humid climates (UAE context). Figure 3 contains two sub-plots: (a) a bar chart comparing annual operational energy savings in USD between two scenarios—G5_55 (5 cm PCM thickness, melting point 55°C) yielding $920.15 and G3_55 (3 cm PCM thickness) yielding $894.29, demonstrating minimal (~2.8%) difference in energy savings despite thickness variation; (b) a Life Cycle Cost (LCC) comparison over PCM's 35-year lifespan, showing G3_55's LCC of $30,699.88 with an extended 30-year payback period, indicating poor financial viability under current UAE electricity pricing. Figure 4 provides a sensitivity analysis visualization comparing three PCM thicknesses (1 cm, 3 cm, 5 cm), plotting cost reduction percentages against energy consumption increase percentages relative to baseline scenarios. Key insight: reducing thickness to 1 cm achieves ~66.67% cost reduction versus 3 cm and ~80% versus 5 cm, while only incurring ~1% energy consumption increase versus G3_55, resulting in a dramatically improved 7-year payback period and LCC of $19,877.99. The charts support the paper's central argument that PCM thickness optimization—not performance—is the primary lever for economic feasibility, and that current PCM pricing in the UAE (averaging $6.35/kg including installation) is the binding constraint to adoption. The visualizations directly substantiate the cost-benefit analysis (Section 3.2) and sensitivity analysis (Section 3.3) findings, reinforcing recommendations for thinner PCM layers and local manufacturing to enable cost-effective passive cooling solutions."}
{"reference_id": "8", "content": "a dynamic thermal response that depends on the material’s temperature and on the absorbed mass of PCM (which depends on the mortar type and PCM).\n\nThese observations are essential for designing materials tailored to specific thermal performance requirements, where the magnitude of thermal inertia and temperature stabilization are critical factors.\n\n4. Conclusions and future work\n\nThis study incorporated three types of phase change materials (PCMs) into different mortars with varying porosities to investigate their impact on thermal, mechanical, and physical properties. A paraffin PCM and two ester PCMs were used in three types of mortars: natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM). The following conclusion were drawn based on the experimental results:\n\n• Using a paraffin PCM with a 21 ◦C melting point and its impregnation in LWAM offered the best solution among all studied in terms of thermal properties. It showed the highest increase in specific heat capacity (42.92 %), maintaining a low conductivity (1.08 W/m⋅K).\n\n• PCM incorporation in mortars leads to a tradeoff between the in crease of the thermal conductivity and the improvement of the thermal inertia. In most cases, the rise in effusivity and specific heat outweighed the increase in conductivity, which is beneficial for reducing energy consumption in buildings. Overall, LWAM showed the best performance since it has the lowest conductivity and highest specific heat capacity after PCM incorporation. However, certain combinations of mortar-PCM might be especially interesting depending on the targeted specific heat and maximum admissible conductivity.\n\n• The interaction between mortar porosity and PCM viscosity plays a decisive role in the incorporated mass of PCM. PCMs with low viscosity are better suited for mortars with lower porosity. In contrast, more viscous PCMs lead to greater incorporation for mortars with a higher porosity.\n\n• PCM addition has a negligible impact on the mechanical properties of NAM and RCAM. In contrast, the mechanical performance of LWAM was improved, indicating that PCM incorporation slightly enhances the strength of porous mortars.\n\n• The incorporation of PCM into mortars altered the transient thermal response. Mortars with PCM exhibit delayed temperature variations due to latent heat exchange during phase transitions. The specific combination of mortar type and PCM led to different thermal responses, indicating that the particular PCM must be carefully chosen depending on the application.\n\nThe experimental findings prove that PCM incorporation into mortars improves thermal performance while maintaining structural integrity, particularly in high-porosity materials like LWAM. This work provides a fundamental understanding of the mechanisms involved in PCM impregnation in cement-based composites. The analysis of the interaction between the aggregate and the PCM type contributes to the development of the incorporation of PCMs into precast elements, such as concrete blocks, which are widely used in construction. This approach ensures controlled impregnation while maintaining structural integrity and optimizing thermal efficiency, allowing for the design of thermal efficient buildings targeted for specific climates.\n\nTo contribute to the deployment of this technology in building en velopes, the following future works are needed:\n\n• Broadening the present work with other PCMs, mortar mixture formulations (altering, for example, the water-to-cement ratio), and incorporation times.\n\n• Measuring the energy storage capacity and the dynamic thermal response of PCM-incorporated mortars applying temperature curves to actual size samples.\n\n• Evaluating the long-term performance of PCM-impregnated mortars under repeated thermal cycling conditions to ensure their durability and stability. Such studies are essential to complement the current findings and validate the practical applicability.\n\n• Advancing the thermal modeling techniques for thermal energy storage mortars. This is especially relevant to study the thermal performance of building envelopes or even whole building systems that incorporate these materials.\n\n• Performing the life cycle and economic analysis of mortars with PCM to evaluate their practical application in sustainable construction.\n\nCRediT authorship contribution statement\n\nLopez-Arias Marina: Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Validation, Software, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Alvarez-Rodriguez Matias: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Software, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Alonso-Martinez Mar: Writing – review & editing, Supervision, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Conceptualization. Velay-Liz ancos Mirian: Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Validation, Supervision, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Conceptualization.\n\nDeclaration of Competing Interest\n\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.\n\nAcknowledgments\n\nThis work was supported by the Spain Ministry of Universities (FPU21/05062 and EST24/00362) and the Spain Ministry of Science and Innovation through Research Projects PID2021-128056OA-I00 and"}
