{"entity": "Cementitious Composites", "type": "concept", "description": "Cementitious composites are a class of cement-based construction composite materials that encompass various types, including cement mortars, steam-cured mortars, Portland cement mortars, and concretes. As fundamental materials in construction, they function as substrates and structural building elements, and they constitute a well-recognized subject domain within scholarly literature on construction materials.

A significant body of research concerning cementitious composites centers on their integration with phase change materials (PCMs). PCMs can be encapsulated or embedded into these composites to improve their thermal performance and enhance their freeze-thaw durability. Notably, cementitious composites serve as the substrate material in studies investigating PCM-based mitigation of freeze-thaw damage. Previous experiments have also demonstrated that PCM incorporation into this class of materials through vacuum techniques can be successfully achieved, with no leaking observed, indicating the feasibility and reliability of this incorporation method.

Beyond direct encapsulation, cementitious composites have been enhanced with PCM-impregnated cork granules, an approach aimed at creating sustainable and energy-efficient building elements. Scholarly articles examining these material systems have investigated them in conjunction with related factors such as PCM types, mortar aggregates, and thermal inertia, reflecting the multidimensional nature of this research area. Overall, cementitious composites represent a material system and subject area frequently discussed in academic documents, particularly those addressing phase change materials and their application to advanced construction and building technologies."}
{"entity": "Phase Change Material (PCM)", "type": "concept", "description": "Phase Change Material (PCM) is a class of functional substances that store and release large amounts of thermal energy as latent heat during reversible phase transitions—typically melting and crystallization—while remaining nearly isothermal at the melting temperature T_m. Key thermophysical parameters include specific heat capacity, latent heat of fusion (defining the Stefan number and total enthalpy), liquid fraction from 0 (fully solid) to 1 (fully liquid), and ~10–15% volumetric expansion upon melting. Their main disadvantage is characteristically low thermal conductivity, motivating enhancement via nanoparticles, metal foams, and fins; in paraffin-based nanocomposites, thermal behavior also depends on nanoparticle dispersion, viscosity, and thermal cycles. Representative materials include paraffin wax, P21, OM42, OM55, RT21-HC, CrodaTherm 19/21, bio-based esters E19/E21, and eutectic KCl solutions.

Acting as latent thermal batteries, PCMs absorb heat nearly isothermally while melting—excess energy from power generation or daytime solar gains—and release it while solidifying, heating air or a coolant when generation is insufficient or sunlight declines, thereby clipping peak temperatures. They form hybrid composites with porous high-silica zeolites such as clinoptilolite (50% loading optimal in one study) and enter lime mortars as microencapsulated slurries or powders; multi-PCM systems (melting points of 18 °C and 25 °C at 20% total dosage) yield controllable thermal activation profiles, though PCM addition raises mortar GWP by ~25–35% and dominates raw-material costs, making selection environmentally and economically critical.

Applications include passive cooling and thermal regulation in building envelopes, facades, glazing, roofs, foamed cement, vacuum-impregnated mortars, and additively manufactured components (melting points 19.8–29.3 °C), with recurring annual savings offsetting high initial investment (UAE glazing: $6.35/kg installed, 35-year lifespan), though roof systems with incomplete overnight solidification achieved only 62% exploitation. PCMs manage lithium-ion pouch-cell battery temperatures in electric vehicles; electronics heat sinks buffer transients, hold devices within ±5 °C, and lower maxima 10–70 °C below ~50 °C thresholds. Further uses include cold-chain fish preservation, pavement subgrade stabilization against frost heave and thaw weakening, and frost-resistant concrete containing PCM-filled steel balls. In active photovoltaic cooling, hot coolant deposits energy into the PCM—potentially the sole cooling path during heat-exchanger bypass—while PV/BIPVT integration damps panel heating and stabilizes electrical output despite minor rear-panel-attachment penalties. In photonics, Sb₂S₃ (melting point 823 K) enables reversible phase switching for SLM/metasurface applications; key design variables (heat capacity, melt temperature, stored energy, mass, and energy bounds) support optimization extendable to batteries and power electronics.

In solar distillation—often termed the "PCM method"—PCM provides latent-heat thermal energy storage: it absorbs solar energy during peak midday radiation via its melting enthalpy and gradually releases the stored latent heat in afternoon/evening hours as radiation declines. This stabilizes feed-water temperature, sustains higher water temperatures and stable operation, extends distillate production duration, and raises solar-to-distillate energy conversion efficiency, significantly enhancing nighttime performance. In specific implementations, the PCM was an orange-colored rectangular block placed within the internal copper trough of one distillation unit, and beeswax served as the PCM in one study. Tubular solar stills with PCM sustained higher freshwater production than non-PCM conditions; quantitatively, daily productivity improved by 11–13% and total system efficiency by 11.47–13.9%.

Notably, the acronym PCM also denotes phase change memory, a distinct technology in which chalcogenide compounds switch via rhombohedral-cubic structural transitions, which should not be confused with the thermal storage material. PCM melting dynamics and novel sustainable PCMs remain active research subjects.<SEP>Materials with high latent heat storage capacity and phase change properties that allow efficient thermal energy absorption and release, making them attractive alternatives to conventional cooling solutions for electronics; examples investigated include RT35, Paraffin Wax, and Salt Hydrate.<SEP>A material that transitions between solid and liquid states, absorbing heat energy as it melts when temperature rises and releasing energy back to the environment as it solidifies when temperature falls, utilizing latent heat for temperature regulation.<SEP>A class of thermal storage materials characterized by low intrinsic thermal conductivity, used in thermal energy storage and electronics cooling applications."}
