2.2.6. Mortar thermal characterization

The thermal properties of the mortar samples (with and without PCM) were determined using the transient plane source (TPS) method. This method uses the Trident Thermal Conductivity Analyzer. A double sided hot disc sensor is placed between two identical samples. Power is applied to the sensor, and it heats up, producing a temperature differ ence in the interface with the samples. This variation is used to measure the thermal conductivity $k ,$ volumetric heat capacity $( \rho C p )$ , and diffusivity α of the samples, complying with the ISO standard 22007–2 [53]. The thermal effusivity of the composite e is calculated according to Eq. (6).

The specific heat capacity of the mortar samples without PCM is determined with Eq. (7), and with PCM, Eq. (8) is used. Since the TPS determines the volumetric heat capacity of the whole composite, it accounts for the solid matrix, air pores, and PCM. In consequence, the bulk density of the samples must be used to calculate the specific heat.

Due to the heterogeneous nature of mortars, small samples required for DSC analysis may not accurately represent the overall thermal response of the material [54]. The complexity of the microstructure in concrete and mortars, characterized by a diverse range of components such as aggregates, cement paste, and air voids, poses challenges that limit the applicability of DSC for thermal analysis. That is why the TPS method was employed alongside heating-cooling tests. They analyze the thermal behavior of the entire mortar sample comprehensively, while they overcome the limitation of DSC for heterogeneous materials.

Additionally, non-standard heating and cooling tests were designed to assess the thermal inertia of the different mortars with and without the different PCMs. For the three types of mortar, 4 samples were studied: the reference sample without PCM and one sample with each PCM. In total, 12 samples were analyzed.

The samples were thermally insulated on each surface except for the top one. To do so, 2.54-cm-thick (1 in) XPS rigid foam was used. These samples were placed in an oven with a constant temperature of $3 5 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ until they reached a uniform and stable temperature. Then, they were removed from the oven and placed inside a cold chamber with a constant temperature of ${ 1 0 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C } }$ for 3 hours. Finally, they were introduced back into the oven at $3 5 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ for 3 hours. This cooling-heating test was repeated 3 times for all samples. Two K thermocouples were placed on each sample: one on the top non-insulated surface and the other on the bottom insulated surface. The thermocouples were connected to a USB-TC 8-channel thermocouple input acquisition device (Measurement

Systems Ltd.). TraceDAQ software (Measurement Systems Ltd.) was used to visualize the temperature readings and to export the data. Fig. 3 shows the setup with the insulated samples, the upper thermocouples, and a scheme of the cross-section showing the location of thermocouples.

These tests measure the transient temperature in the top and bottom surfaces of the samples. The difference in these temperatures illustrates the thermal inertia. Mortars with higher inertia will exhibit more sig nificant differences between top and bottom surface temperatures. These tests also identify the phase change of the incorporated PCMs. Since the thermocouples are placed on the surface of the samples, where the PCM was incorporated, they show the thermal stabilization while the PCMs undergo the phase change process.

3. Results and discussion

3.1. Thermal characterization of the PCMs

The LT-DSC results of the studied PCMs are shown in Fig. 4. The heat flow of the PCM samples measures their transient thermal response during melting and solidification. Bell-shaped curves characterize the phase change process, while the sections with constant heat flow indi cate that the PCM is fully solid or liquid. The temperatures where the bell-shaped curves begin and end denote the phase change temperature range. P21 presents the lowest value of heat flow but also the broadest phase change temperature range (around $1 1 . 5 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ both in melting and solidification). The esters E21 and E19 exhibit narrower phase change ranges (up to $7 . 5 7 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ and $6 . 9 3 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ respectively).

These results also indicate the hysteresis effect in all PCMs. This is the phenomenon where the melting and solidification occur at different temperatures and with unequal thermal response [55].

The ester E21 shows subcooling. This means that its solidification requires an extra drop of temperature to begin (known as the degree of subcooling) [56]. When this happens, after solidification starts, the temperature rises suddenly, which is visible in Fig. 4.

Table 2 shows the phase change properties calculated from the LT DSC results: latent heat, peak temperature, and phase change range. The latent heat of all PCMs is similar, around 200 kJ/kg. The peak temperature is the temperature where the heat flow is maximum. This is a relevant parameter when selecting a specific PCM for a given application. Finally, the phase change range of each PCM is indicated. The effects of hysteresis are observed in the discrepancies between melting and solidification in the peak temperature and the phase change range. The differences are more significant for the esters, complying with previous studies that indicate that hysteresis is more relevant in organic bio-based PCMs"}
{"reference_id": "3", "content": "Impact of the phase change material (PCM) integration on the mortar’s thermal inertia: The role of the aggregates and PCM types

Matias Alvarez-Rodriguez <sup>a,b,\*</sup> , Marina Lopez-Arias <sup>b</sup> , Mirian Velay-Lizancos <sup>b</sup> , Mar Alonso-Martinez <sup>a</sup>

<sup>a</sup> University of Oviedo, GICONSIME Research Group, C/Pedro Puig Adam s/n, Gijon, ´ Asturias 33204, Spain

<sup>b</sup> Lyles School of Civil and Construction Engineering, Purdue University, West Lafayette, IN 47907, USA

A R T I C L E I N F O

Keywords: Mortar Phase change material Thermal inertia Lightweight aggregate Recycled concrete aggregate Cementitious composites Thermal energy storage

A B S T R A C T

Integrating phase change materials (PCMs) into building materials has gained attention for enhancing thermal performance and energy efficiency. However, the impact of different PCM types on cementitious materials thermal and mechanical properties requires further study. This work addresses this gap by incorporating two biobased esters and a paraffin-based PCM into natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM). Thermal and mechanical properties were evaluated using the transient plane source method, heating and cooling cyclic tests, and standard mechanical tests. PCM impregnation was influenced by both PCM viscosity and mortar porosity. High-viscosity PCMs (paraffin) achieved greater absorption in more porous mortars like LWAM, while the incorporation of low-viscosity PCMs (esters) was higher in less porous matrices. LWAM absorbed the highest PCM mass (12.55 g), while NAM and RCAM had higher percentages of filled pores (up to 23.85 % and 28.92 %, respectively). PCM incorporation improved thermal inertia across all mortars, but there was a tradeoff between the rise in conductivity and thermal inertia. LWAM demonstrated the best overall performance, with the lowest thermal conductivity (1.04–1.15 W/m⋅K) and highest specific heat (921.92–1243.10 J/kg⋅K). PCM addition also increased the compressive and flexural strength of LWAM by up to 15 %, with less significant improvements observed in NAM and RCAM. This study’s novelty is its fundamental understanding of the combined effects of PCM viscosity, mortar porosity, and PCM type on thermal and mechanical properties, contributing to the deployment of energy efficient construction materials.

1. Introduction

Reducing greenhouse gas emissions is one of society’s primary goals to minimize the effects of global warming. The International Energy Agency stated that 34 % of final energy consumption is due to the buildings sector, contributing to 37 % of total $\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ emissions worldwide [1], and 40 % of the energy consumption of buildings comes from heating and cooling [2]. Under the current trend, the average surface temperature is expected to rise by $2 . 7 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ in 2100 [3]. In consequence, governments and organizations proposed strategies to reduce energy consumption like the Green Deal [4], the European Climate Law [5], and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development [6].

Latent heat thermal energy storage reduces energy demand, improves the efficiency of HVAC systems, and tackles the mismatch between energy demand and supply [7]. This can be achieved with the incorporation of phase change materials (PCMs) in buildings [8]. PCMs store and release latent thermal energy when they incur in a phase change process, which might be a modification of the state of aggregation (solid-liquid, liquid-gas, solid-gas) or an internal molecular structure rearrangement (solid-solid or liquid-liquid) [7]. Solid-liquid PCMs are preferred for building applications because their latent energy is higher than same-phase PCMs, while the volume variation between the phases is reduced [9]. Thus, PCMs store thermal energy when they are heated and melt, and they release the stored energy when they are cooled and solidify. While PCMs undergo the phase change process, their temperature is almost constant, creating isothermal energy reservoirs, increasing the thermal inertia of building envelopes, reducing energy consumption, and improving thermal comfort inside of the building [10].

PCMs are categorized according to their chemical composition into organic (paraffins and non-paraffins), inorganic (salt hydrates, metals, and alloys), and eutectic mixtures [11]. The desired properties of PCMs for building envelope applications are high latent heat and specific heat capacity, reduced volume changes, non-corrosiveness, non-toxicity, non-flammability, chemical stability, absence of subcooling, and a reasonable price and availability on the market [12]. Although no PCM fulfills all these requirements, organic PCMs are the fittest for con struction applications [13].

The incorporation of PCMs into cementitious materials recently gained attention since they might be used to manufacture active PCM walls, floors, and roofs [14]. However, the phenomenological interactions between PCMs and cement are still not fully understood, and more research is needed [15].

The simplest method is directly incorporating the PCM while mixing cement products, but it is not an effective method due to leakage, chemical reactions, or microstructure modification [16]. Other tech niques were proposed to overcome this, such as shape-stabilization and microencapsulation.

Shape-stabilization consists of containing the PCM in the pores of a supporting material, which is then added to cementitious materials. The supporting materials comprise clay-based powders like diatomite [17] or montmorillonite [18], carbon-based materials like expanded graphene [19] or biochar [20"}
