Analysis: This chart is one of the transient thermal response figures (the Fig. 12–14 series referenced in Section 3.7) from a study on phase change material (PCM) incorporation in mortars, located on the figure-dedicated page between the thermal response discussion and the conclusions (page_idx 4). Based on the strongly defined surrounding context, it is a time-series line plot showing the evolution of temperature measured on the top and bottom surfaces of a mortar specimen — natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), or lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM) — during dynamic thermal testing. Content structure: temperature (°C) on the y-axis versus time on the x-axis, with paired curve sets for top and bottom surfaces, and separate curves for the reference (PCM-free) mortar versus samples impregnated with the paraffin PCM P21 (21 °C melting point) and the ester PCMs (e.g., E19). Key features per the text: (1) all curves tend asymptotically toward 10 °C in the cooling stage and toward 35 °C in the heating stage; (2) the top surface drives convective heat transfer while the bottom surface, insulated by the mortar itself, shows much lower variation and time lag — greater thermal inertia flattens and delays the bottom-surface response; (3) PCM-bearing curves exhibit stabilization plateaus during solidification and melting due to latent heat exchange, with the P21 effect barely noticeable in NAM/RCAM (low PCM mass) but most evident in LWAM with P21 (highest absorbed PCM mass), and E19 showing a faster, more abrupt phase change due to higher initial temperature difference; (4) curve slopes reflect the balance between conductivity (higher k → steeper slope, faster approach to 10/35 °C) and density/specific heat (higher values → slower variation). Relationships: the chart directly demonstrates how mortar type (porosity) and PCM type/absorbed mass govern transient behavior, i.e., creating 'thermally active materials.' Contextual significance: it provides the experimental evidence underpinning the paper's conclusions — PCM addition delays and stabilizes temperature fluctuations; LWAM with P21 offers the highest thermal inertia (42.92 % specific heat capacity increase at low conductivity of 1.08 W/m·K); and mortar–PCM combinations must be matched to the target thermal performance of building envelopes, while unsuitable pairings (e.g., RCAM with P21, where conductivity increase outweighs inertia gain) should be avoided."}
