Analysis: The table titled 'Table 1: Summary of the different types of nanomaterials and their applications' provides a structured overview of 13 distinct nanomaterial types, their specific practical uses, and their corresponding literature references. It is organized into three columns: 'Types of Nanomaterials', 'Used' (the application or functional enhancement), and 'Reference'. A significant pattern emerges showing that thermal management and conductivity enhancement are the dominant applications. Specifically, Carbon Nanotubes, Copper Oxide (CuO), Alumina (Al2O3), Silver (Ag), and Nanofibers are explicitly utilized to enhance thermal conductivity, heat transfer performance, or thermal properties in composite phase-change materials (PCMs). This tabular data directly substantiates the surrounding text's discussion on nano-enhanced phase change materials (NePCMs) and nanoparticle-enhanced phase change materials (NEPCM), which leverage nanoparticles (such as Cu, Cu-Al2O3 hybrids, and carbon-based nanostructures) to improve heat release rates and thermal conductivity. Furthermore, the inclusion of metallic and carbon-based nanomaterials aligns with the text's explanation that metallic systems rely on free electrons for thermal conductivity (accounting for 98–99%), while carbon-based NMs offer superior mechanical and thermal performance. Other materials like Silica (SiO2) specifically target the melting and freezing characteristics of PCMs, while Nano clays and Metal Oxides are applied in coatings and packaging, reflecting the broad utility of nanotechnology across thermal energy storage, electronics, and structural materials."}
