5. Conclusions

The effects of 10% and 20% cement replacement with biomass ash (BA) and the addition of 10% and 20% of PCM on chemical, physical, mechanical and thermal properties of cement–lime pastes were evaluated. The aim was to characterize the paste phase of future mortars for rendering applications with improved sustainability and energy efficiency. The main conclusions of the study were:

A partial replacement of cement with BA did not significantly modify the chemical, physical, mechanical, and thermal properties of the cement–lime pastes, making it a feasible way to improve material sustainability.

The addition of PCM modified the paste porous microstructure, reducing open porosity (accessible to water), fresh entrapped air and capillary water absorption. PCM also reduced mechanical properties such as compressive strength and Ultrasonic Young modulus.

PCM reduced thermal conductivity, being lower for temperatures above PCM melting point, and enhanced the thermal storage capacity of cement–lime pastes.

The combination of 20% of BA and the addition of 20% of PCM showed the best thermal performance with the largest energy storage capacity.

The combination of 20% biomass ash and 20% PCM addition demonstrated synergies for a new sustainable mortar, aiming to a reduction in carbon footprint and improving thermal performance compared to conventional mortars.

