{"entity": "Memory Cell", "type": "artifact", "description": "The Memory Cell is a phase-change random-access memory (PCRAM) cell, an electronic device whose state is set, reset, or read using temperature pulses. Structurally, it is a pre-structured cell consisting of two contact pads and a heating electrode connected to one of the contact pads by a buried tungsten line. The cell contains a switchable phase-change volume and is hosted on a test chip featuring 40 µm contact pads.

Electrically, the memory cell exhibits a minimal resistance of 1 kΩ, while in the amorphous state its resistance can rise to as much as 80 MΩ. All kinds of switching experiments require maximum voltages of 3 V, and the input signal should not exceed 0.15 V. When a voltage is applied to the cell, it is not equal to the voltage drop over the phase-change layer, owing to the electrode resistance. More broadly, memory cells as electronic components display resistances ranging from 500 Ω to 80 MΩ, which the PET measurement setup is specifically designed to handle and characterize; within this setup, different types of memory cells require different contact boards.

Regarding switching behavior, crystallization differences between phase-change materials can affect the switching process, and partial melting followed by quenching leads to cell resistances higher than the minimum SET value. Additionally, shrinking cell dimensions and a surrounding crystalline rim improve the switching speed by enabling direct crystal growth."}
