{"entity": "Electronic Memory", "type": "concept", "description": "Electronic Memory is a fully electronic memory concept based on phase-change materials, first proposed by Neale et al. in 1970. Conceived as a memory element usable in computers or comparable applications, it represents a foundational idea within the broader field of phase-change memory, where it is frequently discussed as a topic addressing memory and storage concepts.

The operating principle of Electronic Memory relies on the marked contrast in resistivity between the amorphous and crystalline states of phase-change materials. This resistance contrast forms the basis for storing information, and stored data is subsequently read out by sensing the material's resistance through the application of voltage pulses.

With respect to material selection, phase-change materials exhibiting fast growth speeds are regarded as candidate materials for electronic memories. Among these, GeTe in particular has been identified as having strong potential for implementation in Electronic Memory, especially in small, growth-dominated devices."}
{"entity": "Chalcogenide", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Chalcogenide refers to a class of chemical compounds and alloys—exemplified by materials such as antimony telluride, Ge2Sb2Te5, and Ge15Sb85—that forms the basis of chalcogenide-based phase-change materials. These materials are defined by two distinct structural states, amorphous and crystalline, which exhibit contrasting electronic structure, bonding nature, and optical/electrical properties. This contrast underlies their technological value, and as amorphous or supercooled-liquid substances, chalcogenides are also characterized by viscosity relations describing their behavior.

Chalcogenides serve as the storage medium at the heart of phase-change memory (PCM) technology within non-volatile memory systems. Their switching kinetics underpin phase-change random access memory, where memory cells undergo rapid amorphous-to-crystalline transitions when triggered by electrical pulses. The material class is central to research on the scalability of phase-change memory and has been studied by prominent researchers including Raoux, Rettner, Burr, and Wuttig, particularly within chalcogenide semiconductor physics. Chalcogenide-based devices, such as the self-rectifying device reported in a 2003 paper, further illustrate the versatility of these materials in practical applications.

Beyond electronic memory, chalcogenide phase-change materials are employed in optical storage media, including DVD and Blu-ray discs. For use in phase-change memory devices targeting automotive, industrial, and military applications, chalcogenide storage materials must possess adequate thermal stability to preserve data integrity under harsh conditions. Furthermore, the crystallization behavior of chalcogenide alloys can be tuned through dopant engineering, notably with aluminum (Al) and copper (Cu), enabling the material properties to be tailored for specific performance requirements across these diverse applications.<SEP>A class of materials, including Sb2S3 or similar compounds, exhibiting phase-change properties used for reconfigurable photonic functionality."}
{"entity": "Phase Change Materials", "type": "artifact", "description": ""Phase Change Materials" (PCMs) refers to two distinct classes of materials that share the same name: (1) thermal energy storage materials used in thermal management engineering, and (2) chalcogenide semiconductors used in electronic, photonic, and data storage applications.

In thermal engineering, Phase Change Materials are substances with a high latent heat of fusion that melt and solidify within a narrow temperature range while storing and releasing substantial amounts of heat. Examples include paraffin waxes, whose melting behavior and phase change characteristics underpin their thermal energy storage capability. These materials are employed for passive thermal management of batteries in electric vehicles, regulating battery thermal behavior and extending heat holding capacity. Implementation approaches include PCM encapsulation pouches, pure encapsulation designs, hybrid liquid cooling configurations, and combinations with heat pipe technology for hybrid thermal management of lithium-ion batteries, with passive approaches studied in contrast to active and hybrid cooling methods. PCMs are also used as passive cooling solutions for photovoltaic systems, absorbing heat and providing temperature drops without requiring energy input, and they find broader application in solar PV cooling, battery management, and green buildings.

In materials science and electronics, Phase Change Materials are a special class of semiconductor materials, most commonly composed of chalcogenide alloys such as GST-124 and GST-225, that can be reversibly switched between amorphous and crystalline phases. These thermally activated transitions are typically induced by laser pulses or controlled thermal pulses. Their exceptional optical and electrical contrast between phases makes them valuable for encoding and rewriting information in data storage, memory devices, and computing applications, as well as in photonics and nanophotonics—including optical switching, the subject of dedicated theses and reports—where their optical properties can be locally modified. PCMs undergo crystallization involving nucleation and crystal growth, and are sub-classified as nucleation-dominated or growth-dominated based on their dominant crystallization mechanism. Crystallization kinetics, cyclability, and the asymmetric crystallization window are central to their switching dynamics. Metavalent bonding plays a central role in this class, and telluride-based compounds exhibit structures amenable to fast phase transitions. These materials are studied using first-principles methods, through which new ternary compositions are predicted from binary parent compounds; additionally, confining heat within the material enhances thermal efficiency and reduces the energy required for phase transition.

"Phase Change Materials" is also the title of a review article by Simone Raoux, published as a book by Springer US in 2009, and cited in the literature (e.g., as reference 28 in source documents)."}
{"entity": "Chalcogenide Glass", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Chalcogenide glass is a class of amorphous glass materials that serves as the foundational material for phase change memory (PCM/PRAM) technology. Its defining property is the ability to undergo reversible phase transitions between amorphous (glassy) and crystalline states. These transitions constitute the physical basis of phase change memory: data is stored by switching the material between these two structural phases, and device operation exploits the distinct intermediate resistance states associated with the material's phase configuration. In this capacity, chalcogenide glass functions as the storage medium of PCM devices.

Beyond its phase-change behavior, thin films of chalcogenide glass also exhibit threshold switching, a characteristic that makes this material foundational not only for phase-change memory but also for ovonic threshold switches and neuromorphic devices.

A representative example of this material class is the compound Ge2Sb2Te5, which belongs to the GeTe-Sb2Te3 (GST) chalcogenide alloy system—one of the most prominent alloy systems used in phase change memory applications. Beyond its technological role, chalcogenide glass is also an active subject of ongoing materials research."}
