{"entity1": "Phase-Change Memory", "entity2": "Resistance Drift", "description": "The relation (Phase-Change Memory, Resistance Drift) captures the fundamental and critical interconnection between phase-change memory (PCM) technology and the phenomenon of resistance drift. Resistance drift is a critical reliability issue and temporal instability phenomenon in PCM devices, in which the resistance of amorphized cells increases over time after programming. It typically occurs after the reset (amorphization) pulse, causing read-level instability, and at elevated temperatures it can be attributed to the spontaneous recrystallization of the amorphous phase.

