Equation: $$
G = U + p V - T S = H - T S,\tag{2.1}
$$
Format: latex

Mathematical Analysis: Equation 2.1 is the fundamental thermodynamic definition of the Gibbs free energy, a state function and thermodynamic potential: G = U + pV - TS = H - TS, where U is the internal energy, p the pressure, V the volume, T the absolute temperature, S the entropy, and H = U + pV the enthalpy. The first equality constitutes a Legendre transformation of the internal energy U(S,V), replacing the natural variables (S, V) with their conjugate intensive variables (T, p). Consequently, its total differential for fixed composition is dG = -S dT + V dp, which proves that G is the correct potential to minimize for spontaneous processes at constant temperature and pressure—precisely the boundary conditions governing phase transitions in condensed matter. The second equality expresses G as enthalpy minus the thermally 'bound' energy TS, i.e., the maximum non-expansion (useful) work extractable from an isothermal, isobaric system. In the surrounding context of phase-change materials (PCMs), the text explicitly designates G as 'the driving force of crystallization': each phase occupies a minimum of G, with the crystalline state being the global (stable) minimum, while the amorphous phase and certain crystalline polymorphs are metastable local minima separated by an energetic activation barrier corresponding to the breaking of inter-atomic bonds. This explains (i) the persistence of the amorphous phase at low temperature (the basis of data retention in phase-change memory cells), (ii) thermally activated crystallization when excitation overcomes the barrier (the SET operation), and (iii) the energetic favorability of the liquid phase above the melting temperature T_melt, enabling amorphization by melt-quenching (the RESET operation). The competition between the enthalpic term H and the entropic term -TS determines relative phase stability: high T amplifies the -TS contribution favoring disordered (liquid/amorphous) configurations, while low T favors the enthalpically optimal ordered crystal. Equation 2.1 is the root expression from which the entire subsequent framework derives: the molar free-energy density difference ΔG_v between amorphous and crystalline phases enters the spherical-nucleus free-energy balance ΔG(r) ∝ (4/3)πr³ΔG_v + 4πr²σ (interface energy σ), whose local maximum defines the critical radius r_c (Eq. 2.3); the critical barrier ΔG_nucleus appears in the Boltzmann factor N_c = N₀ exp(-ΔG_nucleus/k_BT) (Eq. 2.5) for the statistical population of supercritical nuclei; and this feeds into the Vollmer–Weber/Becker–Döring nucleation rate I (Eqs. 2.6–2.8) and the Uhlmann crystalline growth velocity u (Section 2.1.2). Practically, this equation underpins the crystallization and glass-formation kinetics exploited in phase-change memory (PCM), optical storage media, and neuromorphic devices, linking equilibrium thermodynamics to the switching behavior measured in chapter 4."}
