{"entity": "Sb2S3", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Sb2S3, also known as antimony sulfide, antimony trisulfide, or stibnite, is a chemical compound and semiconductive crystal recognized as an emerging wide-bandgap, quasi-1D (low-dimensional) semiconductor and chalcogenide phase-change material (PCM). Characterized by low defect formation energies, it is actively researched for its structure, dynamics, and defect engineering, with applications in photovoltaics and solar cells. It exists in glassy (amorphous, bandgap 2.0 eV) and crystalline (bandgap 1.7 eV) forms that display starkly different surface charging and conductivity, yielding distinctly different electrical and electromechanical behaviors depending on phase.

