{"entity1": "Inverse-Designed Heater", "entity2": "Sb2S3", "description": "The relation (Inverse-Designed Heater, Sb2S3) describes the functional interaction in which an inverse-designed heater serves as the actuation element for electrically switching the wide-bandgap phase-change material (PCM) Sb2S3. In the fully fabricated device, the Sb2S3 layer is positioned atop the inverse-designed heater, which drives its phase transitions. As presented in the associated research, inverse-designed heaters are used to provide this electrical switching of the Sb2S3 phase-change material.

The inverse-designed heater demonstrates reversible, large-area phase switching of Sb2S3. Specifically, it successfully switches a large PCM area of approximately 18×14 μm², contained within an 18×18 μm² target zone, while employing a compact 26×26 μm² heater geometry. The switching is reversible and was sustained over 10 cycles, and it includes amorphization of Sb2S3 within 20 µs at temperatures exceeding the material's 823 K melting point.

To enable amorphization, the heater produces the uniform temperatures (~1000 K) required for this transformation. Notably, it achieves a highly uniform temperature distribution of approximately 1000 K across the switching region, with a temperature variation (ΔT) of only 25 K, ensuring the thermal conditions necessary for reliable and repeatable phase switching of Sb2S3."}
