An advanced superlattice $\left( \mathrm { G e T e / T i  – S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } } \right)$ was investigated during a study in 2023 by Lim et al.. After electrical pulsing, they found that the internal structure of the SL-device was well maintained. This advanced superlattice is characterized by an ultra-low RESET energy $( \sim 2 \mathrm { \ p J ) }$ , high switching speed $( \sim 8 ~ \mathrm { n s } )$ , and high cycling endurance $( \sim 3 * 1 0 ^ { 9 } ) . [ 2 1 ]$

High SL-quality is essential for achieving the desired electrical and structural properties that enable energy-eficient switching. One important indicator of $_ { \mathrm { S L - q u a l i t y } }$ is the presence of well-defined interfaces with van der Waals (vdW)-like gaps between adjacent layers. For GeTe/Sb<sub>2</sub>Te<sub>3</sub> superlattices, previous studies have shown that intermixing between GeTe and $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ thin layers occurs during deposition at the elevated temperatures required for epitaxial growth.[22, 23] Instead of forming the desired superlattice, a heterostructure consisting of $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ and stable GeSbTe (GST) is formed due to thermodynamically driven intermixing at the interfaces.[24]

In this work, high-quality $\mathrm { G e _ { 1 } S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 4 } / I n _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ superlattices are investigated, which were grown by Lucas Bothe at the Helmholtz Nano Facility (HNF) at Forschungszentrum Jülich (FZJ).

2.2. Investigation of $\mathbf { S b } _ { 2 } \mathbf { T e } _ { 3 } ~ / ~ \mathbf { G e } _ { 1 } \mathbf { S b } _ { 2 } \mathbf { T e } _ { 4 }$ superlattices

During her Master’s thesis, Athanasia Piperidou investigated the switching behavior of high-quality superlattices consisting of alternating ultrathin layers of $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ and $\mathrm { G e _ { 1 } S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 4 } }$ . Both of these materials are metavalent PCMs and were reversibly switched between the amorphous and crystalline phases using laser pulses. She analyzed the switching performance of superlattices with varying $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ layer thicknesses and numbers of interfaces and concluded that a lower switching power is required for the superlattice samples compared to conventional GST. Furthermore, she showed a trend towards higher switching eficiency for superlattices with thinner $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ layers and thus thinner supercells. These findings are shown in Fig. 2.7.[10]

Furthermore, the results supported the melt-quench mechanism, as Atom Probe Tomography (APT) revealed a loss of the layered superlattice structure in the amorphous state and its restoration upon recrystallization [10].

In contrast to the electro-thermal confinement hypothesis proposed by Khan et al. [8], she introduced a bond-confinement hypothesis, according to which bondconfinement efects enable the energy-eficient switching of the superlattices. These efects were proposed to become stronger in superlattices with thinner Sb Te layers, potentially explaining the improved switching performance observed for superlattices with thinner supercells [10].

