Analysis: This is Figure 1, a six-panel composite figure (A–F) presenting the device schematic and thermal simulation comparison between a conventional uniformly doped silicon (Si) heater and the paper's inverse-designed Si heater for phase change material (PCM) tuning. Panels (A) and (B) contain device schematics: (A) shows the conventional heater with a uniformly doped active region bridging two metal pads, while (B) shows the inverse-designed heater with a zoomed-in top view (scale bar: 4 µm) revealing the spatially varying width of nanoscale conductive doped-Si filaments connecting the pads. Local filament width modulates electrical resistance, tailoring Joule heating via P = V²/R. Panels (C) and (D) present Lumerical Charge/Heat solver simulated top-view heat profiles over the 26×26 µm² heater area: the conventional heater (C) shows heat concentrated in the central region (edges cooler due to proximity to surrounding Si film), while the inverse-designed heater (D)—with wider, lower-resistance filaments at the boundaries and narrower, higher-resistance filaments at the center—produces a uniform temperature distribution across the dotted-square 18×18 µm² target zone. Panels (E) and (F) show quantitative temperature cross-sections along y = 0: the conventional heater exhibits a spatial temperature variation ΔT of 110 K (between X = 0 and X = 9 µm), whereas the inverse-designed heater reduces this to only 25 K at a ~1000 K target temperature. This figure establishes the theoretical and simulation foundation for the experimental work, demonstrating a 4–5× improvement in thermal uniformity that enables a 10-fold increase in active PCM (Sb₂S₃, melting point 823 K) switching area for spatial light modulator (SLM) architectures."}
