{"entity": "Amorphous Phase", "type": "concept", "description": "The Amorphous Phase is one of the two structural states of phase-change (chalcogenide) materials, contrasting with the crystalline phase in electronic structure, bonding nature, and optical/electrical properties. It is a disordered, glassy, metastable state lacking long-range atomic order, with suppressed atomic mobility and predominantly ordinary covalent bonding. Thermodynamically a metastable local minimum of the Gibbs free energy, it is separated from the crystalline state by an activation barrier (breaking of inter-atomic bonds); as temperature approaches zero, nucleation ceases and it is frozen in metastability. Its very high viscosity keeps it stable at room temperature for decades—underlying data retention in phase-change memory cells—even though it crystallizes rapidly at elevated temperatures.

It typically forms by rapid or ultrafast melt-quenching of the molten state, preventing atoms from ordering into a lattice; it also arises via dc magnetron sputter deposition or the RESET (amorphization) pulse. On Time-Temperature-Transformation diagrams it is the glassy region below the glass transition temperature. In laser processing it appears as dark, opaque spots (dark black/grey circles, lower-left of maps, denoting insufficient recrystallization energy) and is shown blue or red in diagrams. As the pre-crystallization condition (e.g., GST-124), it is the supercooled host matrix (dielectric permittivity ε) in which crystalline nuclei form—spontaneously or field-induced—the whole volume crystallizing once stable nuclei grow, the electric field intensifying in the residual region. Its crystallization kinetics (e.g., Equation 2.3) govern SET switching, and sufficient energy reversibly returns it to the crystalline phase.

Electrically, its disorder prohibits metallic conduction and creates localized trap states, so conduction is thermally activated hopping; resistance falls with rising temperature and exponentially upon annealing below the crystallization temperature. Its resistivity—orders of magnitude above the crystalline phase, exploited for information storage—reaches roughly six orders in GeTe (RESET ~6 MΩ), though cell contrast is ~three orders; full amorphization yields ~1 MΩ, partial amorphization distinct intermediate levels, and optical reflectivity is lower. Material-specific behavior includes AgIn-doped Sb2Te's low amorphous resistivity complicating switching analysis, Ge2Sb2Te5's lying one order below GeTe's, and GeTe's rising as temperature falls, giving high sheet resistance. Even small amorphous volumes—or thin layers above the heating electrode in GeTe cells—raise resistance by orders of magnitude; amorphous volume (resistivity ρ_amo) sets initial switching states, the fresh state defining R₀, undefined at its creation.

Over time, structural relaxation of its disordered network drives power-law resistance drift—resistance rising after programming (e.g., in Ge2Sb2Te5)—with activation-energy drift governing the resistivity; in GeTe, drift energy scales linearly with annealing temperature, and the effective thermal age (sample age τ) encodes relaxation since vitrification. Above a voltage threshold it becomes conductive—the threshold-switching phenomenon. Amorphous GeTe and Ge2Sb2Te5 (defect states studied) and Ge15Sb85 (atomic structure examined) remain active research topics, with transport and structural-change models under development and as-deposited amorphous layers widely used in drift studies.<SEP>One of the two non-volatile structural states of Sb2S3, between which fast and stable state switching occurs under laser irradiation."}
