{"reference_id": "3", "content": "In PCM superlattice studies, the liquidus temperature $\mathrm { T } _ { L }$ is commonly used instead, as superlattices consist of two or more materials, and $\mathrm { T } _ { m }$ is only defined for pure elements. The other characteristic temperature is the crystallization temperature $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ . Below $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ , atomic mobility becomes strongly reduced. If the material has not crystallized before crossing $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ , the structure can become kinetically frozen into a glassy (amorphous) state because atoms no longer possess suficient thermal energy to rearrange into a crystalline structure.

Between $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ and $\mathrm { T } _ { L } ,$ crystallization occurs within the characteristic C-shaped region of the TTT diagram (see Fig. 2.8), whereas outside this region, the system remains in a metastable supercooled liquid state. Starting from either the amorphous or crystalline phase, two possible pathways exist for switching between these states:

2.3.2. Amorphization

When starting from the crystalline phase, amorphization requires heating the PCM above the liquidus temperature $\mathrm { T } _ { L }$ , where the material loses its crystalline order and enters a fully liquid state $\left( \mathrm { T } > \mathrm { T } _ { L } \right)$ . This heating step is one of the main reasons why conventional PCM operation is very energy-intensive, highlighting the relevance of superlattice PCMs, which aim to reduce the required switching energy. The subsequent cooling process below $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ determines whether complete amorphization is achieved:

Ultrafast quenching

If the cooling rate is suficiently high $( \geq 1 0 ^ { 9 } \frac { \mathrm { K } } { \mathrm { s } } ) [ 2 7 ]$ , the melt can be quenched rapidly enough for the cooling trajectory to bypass the nose of the crystallization region in the TTT diagram. Due to the extremely short timescales involved, atomic mobility is too limited for atoms to rearrange into an energetically favorable crystalline structure. Instead, the system freezes into a disordered glassy (amorphous) state, in which atomic mobility is efectively suppressed. This process is referred to as ultrafast quenching (trajectory ○1 in Fig. 2.8) and results in complete amorphization.

Insuficient quenching

If the cooling rate is insuficient to bypass the crystallization region, the cooling trajectory intersects its nose. In this case, atomic mobility remains high enough to enable the formation of small crystalline regions prior to amorphization, resulting in a mixed-phase material that loses its desirable contrast in properties. Therefore, partial recrystallization (trajectory $\textcircled{2}$ in Fig. 2.8) due to insuficient quenching must be avoided in both switching experiments and practical applications.

2.3.3. Crystallization

Starting from the amorphous phase, the initial temperature of the PCM lies below the crystallization temperature $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ . To induce crystallization, the material must be heated into the C-shaped crystallization region of the TTT diagram $( \mathrm { T } _ { L } > \mathrm { T } >$ $\mathrm { T } _ { C } )$ . In contrast to amorphization, where rapid cooling is required, crystallization demands suficiently slow heating and annealing, as the crystallization region is only reached after a characteristic incubation time. Due to the C-shaped form of this region, two distinct annealing pathways enable access to crystallization:

Fast and slow crystallization

Crystallization can occur either on short timescales at temperatures close to the tip of the nose in the TTT diagram (fast crystallization, depicted as trajectory ○3 in Fig. 2.8) or at lower temperatures slightly above the $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ , albeit requiring significantly longer annealing times (slow crystallization, depicted as trajectory ○4 in Fig. 2.8). The origin of these two regimes lies in the temperature dependence of atomic mobility and thermodynamic driving force. While atomic mobility increases with temperature, the free energy diference between the liquid and the crystalline phase, i.e., the thermodynamic driving force for crystallization, decreases. Consequently, increasing the temperature from that required for fast crystallization towards $\mathrm { T } _ { L }$ does not further accelerate crystallization, as the reduced driving force counteracts the enhanced atomic mobility. Conversely, at temperatures close to $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ , the driving force is high, but atomic mobility is limited. Therefore, the fastest crystallization occurs at an intermediate temperature, where an optimal balance between atomic mobility and driving force is achieved.

Moreover, the spontaneous recrystallization of amorphous regions over extended timescales (months to years) at or near room temperature is undesirable and must be considered in memory applications to ensure long-term data integrity.

Nucleation and crystal growth

In addition to these kinetic considerations, crystallization proceeds via two fundamental mechanisms: nucleation and crystal growth, both of which strongly depend on the material system.[16] These mechanisms are illustrated in Fig. 2.9.

For fully amorphous regions, nucleation is required to initiate crystallization. It describes the formation of small crystalline nuclei within an amorphous matrix in the regime of the undercooled liquid. Within the crystallization region of the TTT diagram, numerous such clusters form, making nucleation inherently stochastic due to thermal fluctuations.

Crystal growth, in contrast"}
